.Dd July 4, 2026
.Dt KG 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm kg
.Nd a small text editor with Emacs keybindings
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl QRVh
.Op Ar file ...
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl Q
Do not load the Lisp init file.
Use this to recover from a broken configuration.
.It Fl R
Open all files given on the command line in read-only mode.
.It Fl V
Print version, compiled Lisp feature status, and exit.
.It Fl h
Print a short usage message and exit.
.El
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is a minimal terminal text editor with Emacs-style keybindings.
It supports syntax highlighting for many programming languages,
multiple buffers, split windows, incremental search, a single-entry
kill ring, and undo/redo.
.Pp
.Nm
does not depend on curses; it uses VT100 escape sequences directly.
.Pp
Tabs are displayed with tab stops every eight columns by default, the same
convention as Emacs'
.Li tab-width
default,
.Xr cat 1
and the terminal itself.
The Lisp variable
.Li tab-width
accepts values from 1 through 1000;
.Li setq
changes the default seen by buffers without a local value, while
.Li setq-local
changes the current buffer only.
A tab advances point to the next multiple of that value.
.Pp
Display columns are counted the way the terminal draws them.
East-Asian-Wide and Fullwidth characters (CJK, Hangul, kana, fullwidth
forms and most emoji) occupy two columns, combining marks and
zero-width format characters occupy none, and everything else occupies
one.
The mode line,
.Ic what-cursor-position ,
the goal column kept by
.Ic C-n
and
.Ic C-p ,
rectangle edges and line wrapping all use that same measure, so the
cursor is drawn under the glyph it reports.
The width table follows Unicode 15.1; the combining-mark half of it is
partial and does not cover the Brahmic scripts.
.Pp
Lisp support is compiled in by default and is reported as
.Sq +lisp
by
.Fl V .
A build configured with
.Li WITH_LISP=0
reports
.Sq -lisp .
.Pp
Syntax highlighting has two interchangeable backends chosen at build time,
and
.Fl V
names the one in this binary the same way: the default build reports
.Sq -tree-sitter
and uses the built-in row scanners this page documents, while a build
configured with
.Li WITH_TREE_SITTER=1
reports
.Sq +tree-sitter
and links
.Sy libtree-sitter
from the prefix named by the
.Li TREE_SITTER_PREFIX
make variable.
The tree-sitter backend loads grammars at run time by soname
.Pq Pa libtree-sitter- Ns Ar name Ns Pa .so ,
searched along
.Ev KG_TS_GRAMMAR_PATH ;
it highlights C, Python, YAML, Markdown, JavaScript, React/JSX, TypeScript,
TSX, Java, Rust, Go, HTML, Emacs Lisp, Makefile and Shell, and treats
every other buffer as plain text.
Markdown uses the block grammar only, so headings, code fences, block quotes
and list markers are coloured while inline markup inside a paragraph is not.
There is no language injection: the JavaScript inside an HTML
.Ic <script>
element and the shell inside a Makefile recipe are plain text, while the
tags and
.Ic $(...)
references around them are not.
The TypeScript mode chooses its grammar from the file name, since
.Pa .tsx
and
.Pa .ts
are separate tree-sitter grammars that mis-parse each other's files.
A mode whose grammar is missing is not an error and never falls back to the
row scanners.
The mode names, mode commands and file-name detection below are unaffected
by either choice.
.Pp
On startup
.Nm
loads the Lisp init file
.Pa $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/kg/init.el ,
falling back to
.Pa ~/.config/kg/init.el ,
unless
.Fl Q
is given.
A missing init file is normal.
On a load error the
.Li FILE:LINE
diagnostic is shown in the status area and
forms evaluated before the error remain applied.
Init files and packages are trusted code: they run with the full
privileges of the editor process.
This is not a sandbox; see
.Pa doc/lisp-api.md
for the full reference (object lifetimes, position units, callback
ordering, error handling) behind this section's narrative tour.
.Pp
An empty buffer shows the startup screen: a centred logo and slogan.
.Nm
reads
.Li inhibit-startup-screen
once, after the init file has run, and draws no startup screen when it is
non-nil.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
(setq inhibit-startup-message t)
.Ed
.Li inhibit-startup-message
is Emacs' other spelling of the same switch and either name suppresses
the screen.
Unlike Emacs, where a
.Li defvaralias
makes the two names one variable, kg's are two variables, so setting one
does not change what the other reads back.
Neither suppresses the
.Dq Press Ctrl-h for help
greeting in the status area.
.Fl Q
loads no init file, so both stay
.Li nil
and the startup screen is shown.
.Pp
Packages load explicitly with
.Li (load "name") ,
which resolves a bare name to
.Pa <config>/kg/lisp/name.el
and treats names containing a slash as literal paths.
A bare name may be written with or without the
.Pa .el
suffix; both spellings resolve to the same file, as in Emacs.
Loading a file twice with
.Li load
evaluates it twice, and
.Li load
answers
.Li t
when it succeeds.
An error raised by a loaded file \(em at read time or run time, and
including the nesting-depth limit and a missing file \(em is caught by a
.Li condition-case
written around the
.Li load ,
with its original condition symbol.
A file that is not there raises Emacs' own
.Li file-missing
\(em
.Li ("Cannot open load file" "No such file or directory" PATH)
as its data, and the same condition and data from
.Li require
when nothing in the load-path matches \(em so a handler may name
.Li file-missing ,
its parent
.Li file-error ,
or
.Li error .
A permission failure raises the parent class where Emacs raises its third
leaf
.Li permission-denied ,
which kg records rather than implements.
A
.Li throw
out of a loaded file reaches a catch around the
.Li load ,
as in Emacs: loaded forms are read and evaluated one at a time in the
caller's own run, so errors, throws and quits cross the
.Li load
as if the forms were written in place.
.Li (require FEATURE &optional FILENAME)
loads at most once, a no-op when
.Li (provide FEATURE)
has already run, and errors ("cyclic require") when re-entered for a
feature already mid-load.
.Li (featurep FEATURE)
asks without loading.
.Li require
resolves
.Li FILENAME
(or
.Li FEATURE Ns 's
own name) through
.Li load-path ,
a bounded array of directories \(em not a Fe list \(em defaulting to one
entry,
.Pa <config>/kg/lisp/ ,
searched in order, first match wins.
.Li FILENAME
follows the same suffix rule
.Li load
applies to a bare name: it may be written with or without the
.Pa .el
suffix and both spellings resolve to the same file, while a name
containing a slash is a literal path, neither suffixed nor searched.
.Li (add-to-load-path DIR)
prepends a directory so it is searched first.
.Li (autoload SYMBOL FILE ...)
is accepted and inert.
Emacs arms lazy loading with it \(em the first call loads
.Li FILE
\(em while kg records nothing and loads nothing, so a function that only
an autoload would have provided is
.Li void-function
at its first call and
.Li fboundp
answers nil for it.
The form exists so that a package's own header does not end the load
before its first definition.
.Pa lisp/auto-fill.el
is a worked package: it
.Li provide Ns s
.Sq auto-fill ,
and
.Li (auto-fill-mode)
after
.Li (require 'auto-fill)
breaks lines at
.Li fill-column
as they are typed past it, via
.Li after-change-functions
and one
.Li replace-region
call per break.
.Li fill-column
is the prelude's own variable, 70 by default, shared with
.Li fill-region
and
.Ic M-q ;
a package does not have to be loaded for the editor to have a fill
column.
Its hook entry point is a
.Li condition-case
that removes the hook and reports once, so a bad
.Li fill-column
disables the mode instead of erroring on every keystroke.
.Pp
.Pa lisp/grep-buffer.el
is the package to read first if you want to write one.
.Li (require 'grep-buffer)
then
.Li grep-buffer
asks for a regexp, collects every matching line of the current buffer
into
.Pa *grep-buffer*
\(em one
.Li "LINE: TEXT"
row per hit, with the matched text in brackets and a count at the end
\(em and shows it.
With point on a row,
.Li grep-buffer-goto
returns to the line that row names;
.Li grep-buffer-word-at-point
searches for the word under the cursor, and
.Li grep-buffer-ignore-case
folds case.
It is not the built-in
.Li occur ,
which is C and integrates with
.Ic M-g M-n ;
it is Lisp a user can change.
.Pp
.Pa lisp/help-fns.el
is the discovery surface.
.Li (require 'help-fns)
adds
.Li describe-function ,
.Li describe-variable
and
.Li apropos ,
which report into
.Pa *Help* .
.Li describe-function
names what a command or function is, how it is called interactively, and
what its documentation says;
.Li describe-variable
reports a variable's value, whether the value you are seeing is this
buffer's own or the global one, and whether
.Li let
binds it dynamically;
.Li apropos
lists every name containing a substring.
What
.Li apropos
can see is every interned symbol plus the built-in command table, capped
at
.Li apropos-max-results
matches per report.
.Pp
.Nm
installs those packages to
.Pa <prefix>/share/kg/lisp/
.Pq Pa /usr/local/share/kg/lisp
by default .
That directory is
.Em not
on the default
.Li load-path ,
which is the per-user
.Pa <config>/kg/lisp/
alone, so an
.Pa init.el
reaches it with
.Li (add-to-load-path "/usr/local/share/kg/lisp")
before the
.Li (require 'auto-fill) ,
or copies the file into
.Pa <config>/kg/lisp/
instead.
The prelude is deliberately not installed as a file: it is compiled into
the binary, so it can never be missing or a version behind the editor
that evaluates it.
.Pp
Lisp code defines interactive commands the way Emacs does, with
.Li defun
plus
.Li (interactive) ,
and binds them with
.Li (global-set-key "C-c <key>" "name") :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
(defun insert-date ()
  "Insert today's date."
  (interactive)
  (insert "2026-07-04"))
(global-set-key "C-c d" "insert-date")
.Ed
.Pp
An
.Li (interactive ...)
form in the declaration position \(em immediately after an optional
docstring, and nowhere else \(em is removed from the function body and
registers it under its own symbol, through the function cell.
A later
.Li (interactive ...)
stays an ordinary, nil-valued body form.
.Li (commandp NAME)
answers whether a name is a command.
The underlying registry is also reachable directly as
.Li (define-command NAME FN &optional SPEC DOC) ,
where NAME is a symbol or a string, FN a function value, SPEC nil, a
specification string, or a zero-argument function returning the argument
list, and DOC nil or a string.
Defined commands appear in M-x completion and run under the normal
evaluation budget;
.Li remove-command
and
.Li global-unset-key
undo the registrations.
Only two-key sequences starting with C-c are bindable:
C-c is reserved for user bindings and is otherwise undefined.
.Pp
Buffer positions follow Emacs: a position is a 1-based codepoint offset,
so
.Li (point-min)
is 1,
.Li (point-max)
is one past the last character, and each line break counts as one
character.
Offsets count characters rather than bytes.
The position and mark API is
.Li (point) ,
.Li (goto-char N) ,
.Li (goto-line N) ,
.Li (point-min) ,
.Li (point-max) ,
.Li (line-number-at-pos) ,
.Li (current-column)
(a display column, so tabs expand and wide characters count two),
.Li (mark) ,
.Li (set-mark N) ,
.Li (deactivate-mark) ,
.Li (region-beginning) ,
.Li (region-end) ,
.Li (buffer-substring BEG END) ,
.Li (char-after &optional POS) ,
.Li (forward-word &optional N) ,
.Li (backward-word &optional N)
and
.Li (bounds-of-thing-at-point THING) .
Out-of-range positions clamp to the buffer;
.Li buffer-substring
accepts its bounds in either order; and
.Li region-beginning
and
.Li region-end
signal an error when no mark is set.
.Li set-mark
also activates the region, so there is no separate activate-mark.
.Li goto-line
moves point to the beginning of the line and takes no column, as in Emacs;
reach a column from there with
.Li (goto-char (+ (point) N)) .
.Pp
.Li bounds-of-thing-at-point
supports
.Li 'word
and
.Li 'line
and returns a cons
.Li "(START . END)"
of positions, or nil when point is not on such a thing; any other symbol
raises an error.
Both things are bounded as Emacs bounds them.
Point just after a word still belongs to that word and point between words
belongs to none;
.Li 'line
runs from the start of the line to the start of the next one, so it
includes the line break, and on the last line it ends at
.Li (point-max) .
Every codepoint from U+0080 up counts as a word constituent there, so
accented and CJK words come back whole.
The interactive word commands
.Pq M-f , M-b , M-@ , M-d , M-t
and the Lisp
.Li forward-word
and
.Li backward-word ,
which drive them, remain ASCII-only.
.Pp
Editing and search go through the same gateway every command does, so each
of these is one undo step:
.Li (delete-region START END) ,
.Li (replace-region START END TEXT) ,
.Li (fill-region START END) ,
.Li (search-forward STRING &optional BOUND) ,
.Li (search-backward STRING &optional BOUND) ,
.Li (re-search-forward PATTERN &optional BOUND) ,
.Li (re-search-backward PATTERN &optional BOUND) ,
.Li (match-beginning N) ,
.Li (match-end N) ,
.Li (make-marker) ,
.Li (set-marker MARKER POS &optional BUF)
and
.Li (marker-position MARKER) / (marker-buffer MARKER) .
.Li fill-region
fills EVERY paragraph between the two positions, at
.Li fill-column ,
one undo step per paragraph, and answers the fill prefix -- the last
filled paragraph's indent,
.Li \(dq\(dq
when it had none, and
.Li nil
when the region held no paragraph at all.
It is the fill
.Ic M-q
runs and has that fill's two limits: no
.Li sentence-end-double-space
rule (kg has no sentence machinery, so a period followed by one space is
an ordinary break point where Emacs refuses one), and a column counted in
bytes rather than in display cells, so non-ASCII text wraps early.
.Pp
None of the search natives wrap around the buffer the way
.Ic C-s / C-r
do, none fold case, and a match cannot span two lines.
.Li re-search-backward Ns 's
notion of the match in a line holding more than one is not Emacs' bounded
backward search; see
.Pa src/lisp_search.c
for the exact rule.
Match data (what
.Li match-beginning
and
.Li match-end
read) outlives the search that set it, the same way point outlives a
.Li goto-char ,
but
.Li set-buffer
lasts only for the top-level form that calls it: the next command starts
again in the window's buffer, and
.Li with-current-buffer
is how to scope it explicitly.
Point belongs to the buffer rather than to the running form, so a buffer
remembers where Lisp left point in it even while no window shows it; only
the buffer the form started in is synchronised back to the window, and
only when the form returns without an error.
.Pp
.Li (save-excursion BODY...)
and
.Li (with-current-buffer BUF BODY...)
restore what they saved on every exit, including an error or
.Ic C-g .
.Pp
.Li (add-hook 'HOOK FN &optional LOCAL) ,
.Li (remove-hook 'HOOK FN)
and
.Li (run-hooks 'HOOK)
manage hooks, which run at a safe point after the event rather than inside
the edit that caused it; one failing hook neither stops the others nor
disturbs the form that was running.
The hooks that exist are
.Li after-change-functions ,
.Li find-file-hook ,
.Li before-save-hook ,
.Li after-save-hook ,
.Li kill-buffer-hook
and
.Li post-command-hook .
Every hook runs in the buffer it is about.
.Li kill-buffer-hook
runs while the buffer is still live and after every refusal has been
passed, so a hook body can still read what is about to be lost; it may
not kill the buffer it is being run for.
.Li post-command-hook
runs once per keystroke the editor has finished processing rather than
once per command, since
.Nm
has no
.Li self-insert-command .
.Pp
Variables can be buffer-local.
.Li (setq-local SYM VAL)
gives the current buffer a binding of its own,
.Li (setq-default SYM VAL)
and
.Li (set-default 'SYM VAL)
write the value buffers without one see, and
.Li (default-value 'SYM)
reads it past whatever binding is in force.
.Li (make-local-variable 'SYM)
creates a binding seeded from the default,
.Li (kill-local-variable 'SYM)
drops it,
.Li (local-variable-p 'SYM &optional BUFFER)
asks whether one exists and
.Li (buffer-local-value 'SYM BUFFER)
reads another buffer's without selecting it.
An ordinary reference reads the current buffer's binding when it has one
and the default otherwise, a plain
.Li setq
writes whichever is in force, and a binding dies with its buffer.
A
.Li let
over such a name binds the binding in force where it runs, and must be
left in the buffer it was entered in.
.Li FN
may be a function value or a quoted symbol naming one, as in Emacs:
.Li (add-hook 'my-hook 'my-fn) .
A symbol is resolved when the hook runs, not when it is added, so
redefining the function afterwards takes effect.
.Pp
.Nm
tracks up to 8 child processes at once, started from Lisp with
.Li (start-process NAME BUFFER PROGRAM &rest ARGS)
(exec'd directly, no shell) or
.Li (start-shell-command NAME BUFFER COMMAND)
(through
.Pa /bin/sh Fl c ,
the way
.Ic M-!
does).
.Li (process-live-p PROC) ,
.Li (delete-process PROC)
(SIGTERM, a bounded wait, then SIGKILL),
.Li (process-buffer PROC) ,
.Li (set-process-filter PROC FN) ,
.Li (set-process-sentinel PROC FN)
and
.Li (process-status PROC)
(answering
.Li run , exit
or
.Li signal )
round out the API.
.Li BUFFER
is a buffer object, a name resolved the way
.Li get-buffer-create
resolves one, or
.Li nil
to discard the process's output; a process object is a generation-checked
handle into the table, never a PID, so a handle to a reclaimed slot never
resolves to whatever process claims it next.
Setting a filter stops output from landing in
.Li process-buffer
\(em the filter owns it, exactly as in Emacs \(em and clearing it with
.Li (set-process-filter PROC nil)
resumes appending.
Both callbacks run only at a safe point, and a filter always sees
everything a process wrote before its sentinel runs; a filter or sentinel
that errors is reported in the status area without stopping the other one,
another process's callbacks, or later deliveries.
Children get pipes, not a pty, and
.Pa /dev/null
on stdin; there is no
.Li process-send-string .
Output is capped at 256 KiB per process, oldest bytes dropped past that
with a
.Li kg: output truncated
marker, and every tracked process is killed and reaped when
.Nm
exits.
.Pp
.Li (define-key MAP KEY COMMAND) ,
.Li (lookup-key MAP KEY)
and
.Li (current-local-map)
reach the same layered keymaps the built-in keys use.
Map names are
.Nm Ns 's
own
.Pq Li global , dired , compilation ,
and the Emacs spellings
.Li global-map
and
.Li dired-mode-map
resolve to them.
.Li lookup-key
reports what the named map says whether or not it is active, which is not
the same question as what the next keystroke will do.
Unlike
.Li global-set-key ,
.Li define-key
takes any sequence, so it can shadow a built-in binding;
.Ic C-g
and
.Ic C-x C-c
are the keys to leave alone.
.Pp
Upstream fe has no string operations; the string natives are provided by
.Nm
itself, as is the prelude written in Fe and evaluated at startup.
The prelude is where the Emacs Lisp surface lives; see
.Pa doc/fe-upstream.md
for the short list of changes that had to be made in the
.Pa fe
submodule itself.
The string API is
.Li (string-length S) ,
.Li (substring S FROM &optional TO) ,
.Li (concat A B ...) ,
.Li (string= A B) ,
.Li (char-to-string N) ,
.Li (string-to-char S)
and
.Li (format FORMAT ARG ...) .
Indices count codepoints rather than bytes, so no result is cut
mid-glyph.
.Li substring
takes 0-based indices, counts negative ones from the end, clamps
out-of-range ones, and yields the empty string when TO precedes FROM.
.Li concat
accepts any number of arguments and
.Li (concat)
is the empty string.
.Li char-to-string
is the inverse of
.Li char-after ,
which returns a number; it rejects 0, surrogates and values above
U+10FFFF.
.Li string-to-char
returns nil for the empty string.
.Pp
.Li format
substitutes the specifiers Emacs Lisp reaches for most:
.Li %s
prints an object the way the interpreter prints it, so a string prints
bare and a list prints as a list;
.Li %S
is the same with strings quoted, and with a quoted form abbreviated the
way Emacs abbreviates it, so
.Li (format \(dq%S\(dq \(aq\(aqx)
is
.Li \(dq\(aqx\(dq ;
.Li %d
accepts either number type, printing an integer exactly and truncating a
float toward zero;
.Li %e , %f
and
.Li %g
are the C floating-point conversions, accepting either number type as
they do in Emacs; and
.Li %%
is a literal per cent.
Numeric and string conversions accept field widths, precision, and the
.Li -
and
.Li 0
flags.  Emacs' remaining flags
.Li + ,
a space and
.Li # ,
and its
.Li N$
field numbers, are not accepted: each raises
.Li invalid format operation
rather than being read as something else.  The additional conversions are
.Li %c , %x , %X
and
.Li %o ;
.Li %c
renders a valid codepoint as UTF-8, and refuses 0, where Emacs writes a
NUL byte \(em nothing
.Nm
stores in a string may contain one.
Extra arguments are ignored, as in Emacs, while a missing argument, an
unknown specifier and a format string that ends inside one all raise an
error.
NaN and the infinities have no integer to print, so
.Li %d
refuses them;
.Li %e , %f
and
.Li %g
print them the way C spells them, which is the spelling Emacs uses for
those specifiers too;
.Li %s
and
.Li %S
print them in the interpreter's own readable float syntax, as
.Li 1.0e+INF
and
.Li -0.0e+NaN ,
matching Emacs.
.Pp
The prelude is available before any init file is loaded.
.Pp
.Li defcustom
is a declaration over
.Li defvar :
it initializes only an unbound global, records its docstring, and accepts
the inert presentation keywords
.Li :type , :options , :group , :tag , :link , :version
and
.Li :package-version .
.Li custom-set-variables
accepts only quoted
.Li (SYMBOL VALUE)
entries.  Customize state, semantic metadata and a Customize UI are not
provided.  A
.Li declare
form immediately after a definition's optional docstring is an accepted
no-op.
.Pp
The prelude defines the definition forms
.Li defun ,
.Li defmacro ,
.Li defvar ,
.Li defconst ,
.Li defcustom ,
.Li custom-set-variables ,
.Li declare
and
.Li interactive ;
the binding forms
.Li (let ((VAR VALUE) ...) BODY ...) ,
.Li let* ,
.Li progn
and
.Li (special-variable-p \(aqNAME) ;
.Pq Li setq
and
.Pq Li set
are core Fe special forms, not prelude definitions, and
.Li =
is numeric equality, chained over any number of arguments, alongside
.Li < ,
.Li <= ,
.Li > ,
.Li >=
and
.Li /=
(the whole comparison family is core Fe, not prelude definitions);
it was Fe's assignment operator in earlier versions of
.Nm ,
so Lisp written against those needs
.Li setq
instead;
the control forms
.Li (cond (TEST BODY ...) ...) ,
.Li (when COND BODY ...) ,
.Li (unless COND BODY ...) ,
.Li prog1 ,
.Li (dolist (VAR LIST &optional RESULT) BODY ...)
and
.Li (dotimes (VAR COUNT &optional RESULT) BODY ...) ;
the non-local exits
.Li (catch TAG BODY ...) ,
.Li (throw TAG VALUE) ,
.Li (condition-case VAR BODY (CONDITION HANDLER ...) ...) ,
.Li (signal \(aqSYMBOL DATA) ,
.Li (error "FORMAT" ARG ...) ,
.Li (unwind-protect BODY CLEANUP ...)
and
.Li (ignore-errors BODY ...)
(all core Fe forms except
.Li ignore-errors ,
the prelude's macro over
.Li condition-case ) ;
the list library
.Li length ,
.Li nth ,
.Li nthcdr ,
.Li last ,
.Li reverse ,
.Li append ,
.Li mapcar ,
.Li mapc ,
.Li mapconcat ,
.Li assoc ,
.Li assq ,
.Li member ,
.Li memq ,
.Li push ,
.Li pop ,
.Li nreverse ,
.Li delq ,
.Li delete ,
.Li add-to-list ,
.Li caar ,
.Li cadr ,
.Li cddr ,
.Li 1+
and
.Li 1- ;
the small library
.Li identity ,
.Li prog2 ,
