.Pp
kg's Lisp differs from Emacs Lisp in ways worth knowing:
variables are lexical by default, while
.Li defvar
and
.Li defconst
mark a symbol special and a
.Li let
over a marked name binds it dynamically \(em so the ordinary Emacs
temporary-setting idiom works, the binding swaps the symbol's global
value and restores it on every way out including an error, a
.Li throw
and
.Li C-g ,
.Li (special-variable-p \(aqNAME)
answers whether a name was marked, and a lambda or defun parameter
named after a special binds lexically as it does in Emacs;
a one-argument
.Li (defvar v)
is scoped to the reader/evaluation unit it appears in, as in Emacs, so a
later file's
.Li let
over the same name is lexical again \(em the one residual is a
.Li defun
written after such a
.Li defvar
and called from another file, which Emacs keeps dynamic and kg answers
lexically;
what kg does not have is automatically buffer-local variables
\(em
.Li make-variable-buffer-local
and
.Li defvar-local
are absent, so a plain
.Li setq
never creates a buffer-local binding where Emacs' would
\(em
or whole-file
.Li lexical-binding: nil
semantics;
the printer abbreviates
.Li (quote X)
back to
.Li \(aqX
as Emacs' does, while backquote forms still print the long way because
kg's reader expands them to the ordinary symbols
.Li quasiquote , unquote
and
.Li unquote-splicing ,
which is measured blocked rather than merely unfixed:
.Li \(ga
and
.Li ,
are reader delimiters and symbol escapes are a deliberate read error, so
after a rename the prelude could not spell the macro it defines;
.Li (eval FORM)
\(em by contrast with the callback walls above, and matching Emacs \(em
evaluates FORM in the caller's own run, so a condition, a
.Li throw
or a
.Li C-g
out of it reaches the enclosing handler, and its environment is the
global one, which is Emacs' answer for a nil LEXICAL argument;
.Li eq
is Emacs' eq: integers compare by value, while separately-read equal
strings and float objects do not;
numbers are signed 64-bit integers or doubles, so integer overflow and
integer division by zero raise an
.Li arith-error
message rather than promoting to a bignum;
.Li t ,
.Li nil
and keyword symbols are protected constants, refused by
.Li setq ,
.Li set ,
a
.Li let
binding name,
.Li fset
and
.Li defalias
with the
.Li setting-constant
condition, and keywords are self-evaluating, which
.Li keywordp
reports \(em a lambda parameter may still shadow
.Li t ,
as in Emacs;
values and functions live in separate namespaces as in Emacs, so call
position reads only the function cell;
character literals such as
.Li ?a
read as their codepoint numbers, and
.Li #x , #o
and
.Li #b
read radix integers;
.Li condition-case
and
.Li catch / throw
exist with a static condition hierarchy:
.Li wrong-type-argument , void-function , arith-error , setting-constant
and others are under
.Li error ;
.Li quit
is a separate branch not catchable by
.Li (error \&...) ;
.Li unwind-protect
runs its cleanup forms on any non-local exit \(em a normal return, an
error, a
.Li throw ,
.Li C-g ,
or budget exhaustion \(em and a
.Li condition-case
written inside a cleanup handles what that cleanup raises, leaving
whatever was being unwound in flight, while a cleanup error nothing in
the cleanup handles still replaces that completion as it does in Emacs
\(em two corner shapes of where the replacement lands diverge, recorded
as the compat manifest's
.Li cleanup-raise-residuals
row;
.Li save-excursion
and
.Li with-current-buffer
are transparent to the enclosing evaluation, throw included, so an error
inside either body reaches a
.Li condition-case
written around the form with its original condition, a
.Li throw
reaches a catch written around the form with the value it threw, and the
restore has already run by the time either does, while a hook or
process-callback boundary contains an error
(and a
.Li throw
naming a catch outside the callback) rather than letting one broken hook
stop the editor's evaluation \(em though a
.Li C-g
or a budget exhaustion is never contained and cancels the whole
evaluation;
a
.Li throw
out of a hook, a process filter or sentinel, or a nested
.Li command-execute
still does not reach the catch that names its tag (kg's
native-reentry wall, which those callbacks remain) but becomes
.Li (no-catch TAG VALUE) ,
which an enclosing
.Li condition-case
handles;
kg's own editor natives still signal a plain
.Li error
whose message reads like a condition name, so a handler naming the
specific symbol does not match one of them \(em classifying them is the
follow-up this phase deferred;
the namespace diagnostics
.Li void-function ,
.Li void-variable
and
.Li cyclic-function-indirection ,
and the numeric
.Li arith-error ,
are now catchable condition objects;
a macro's function cell holds fe's own macro object, not Emacs'
.Li (macro . FUNCTION)
cons (visible only through
.Li (symbol-function \(aqa-macro) ) ;
and Lisp nesting (recursive calls, nested special forms, self-expanding
macros) is bounded by the interpreter's frame stack, not by C or
garbage-collector recursion — kg's default arena holds about 540
levels of ordinary self-recursion (measured 544 at the Phase 12 fix
cycle).
A native re-entering the evaluator synchronously, as a hook function, a
process filter or sentinel and a nested
.Li command-execute
do, has its own, much smaller bound (32 nested re-entries), since each
level there is a real C stack frame.
.Li save-excursion
and
.Li with-current-buffer
were the example here until they became prelude macros over
.Li unwind-protect ;
they no longer re-enter, which is why a
.Li throw
out of either now reaches a
.Li catch
outside it.
A structure that refers to itself prints as far as the cycle and then
.Li #<cycle> ,
rather than being printed forever.
.Pp
The editor bridge uses the Emacs names
.Li insert ,
.Li message ,
.Li buffer-name ,
.Li load ,
.Li global-set-key
and
.Li global-unset-key .
.Li message
formats, taking the same specifiers as
.Li format ,
so a literal per cent in a message has to be written
.Li %% .
Built-in editor commands run with
.Li (command-execute \(aqname) ,
which takes a quoted symbol as in Emacs or equivalently a string, never
passes a prefix argument outside dispatch, and is limited to the commands
.Nm
allows Lisp to call.
.Li define-command
and
.Li remove-command
have no Emacs equivalent: they are the name-to-function registry that
.Nm
keeps, and that
.Li (interactive)
inside a
.Li defun
writes to.
A command defined this way counts as a command that edits the buffer, so a
read-only buffer refuses it before it runs, exactly as it refuses
.Ic M-x upcase-word ;
a
.Li defun
recognises only an
.Li (interactive ...)
form immediately after an optional docstring.
The supported codes are
.Li p ,
.Li P ,
.Li r ,
.Li s ,
.Li n ,
.Li N ,
.Li f ,
.Li F ,
.Li b
and
.Li B ;
they deliver prefixes, sorted region bounds, literal strings, numbers, paths,
and buffer names without visiting or selecting the result.
Instead of a specification string the declaration may carry a single
form, as in
.Li (interactive (list 1 2)) ,
which is evaluated in the command's own lexical environment at invocation
time and must return a proper list of arguments.
A valid Emacs code kg has not implemented, and the deferred modifiers
.Li * ,
.Li @
and
.Li ^ ,
report
.Dq unsupported interactive code ;
a byte outside Emacs' set reports
.Dq invalid interactive code .
In both cases the command body does not run.
.Pp
The raw prefix is temporarily available as
.Li current-prefix-arg ,
and
.Li prefix-numeric-value
converts it: nil is 1, an integer is itself, a one-element list is its
element, and the symbol
.Li -
is \-1.
The binding is made and unmade at the command boundary by kg's C rather
than by a
.Li let
over a
.Li defvar Ns 'd
name, and
.Li current-prefix-arg
is not marked special, so a
.Li let
over that name is an ordinary lexical binding: the forms lexically
inside the
.Li let
read it, and a function called from inside still reads the
command-boundary value \(em
.Li (let ((current-prefix-arg 7)) (list current-prefix-arg (f)))
measures
.Li (7 nil) .
Prompts are literal \(em a
.Li %
in prompt text is not a format directive, which is a recorded divergence
\(em and are available only while running a command from a real key or M-x
context; from an init file, a hook, a process callback, or
.Ic M-:
without a command, prompting fails before reading input.
Argument construction is strict and capped at 16 arguments, itself a
recorded divergence rather than a silent truncation.
.Li n
always prompts and ignores any prefix;
.Li N
returns a supplied prefix without prompting.
A number must be one decimal token \(em an optional sign, digits, an
optional fraction and an optional exponent \(em with nothing but
whitespace around it; anything else, including
.Li inf ,
.Li nan ,
.Li 0x10
and an empty answer, re-prompts.
Cancelling any prompt with
.Ic C-g
is a quit and aborts before the command body runs; an answer too long for
the prompt buffer is refused rather than truncated \(em an error for the
Lisp codes, and for
.Ic C-x C-f
and the other interactive path prompts a dismissal with
.Dq Path too long
in the echo area.
kg supplies no public
.Li read-*
functions and no completion framework.
.Pp
If no files are given,
.Nm
opens a scratch buffer.
Multiple files each open in their own buffer.
.Sh KEY BINDINGS
.Ss Navigation
.Bl -column "C-Home / C-End" "XXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXX" -compact
.It Sy Key Ta Sy Action
.It C-f / \&\(-> Ta Move forward one character
.It C-b / \&\(<- Ta Move backward one character
.It C-n / \(da Ta Move to next line
.It C-p / \(ua Ta Move to previous line
.It C-a / Home Ta Move to beginning of line
.It C-e / End Ta Move to end of line
.It C-v / PgDn Ta Scroll down one page
.It M-v / PgUp Ta Scroll up one page
.It M-f / C-\(-> Ta Move forward one word
.It M-b / C-\(<- Ta Move backward one word
.It M-e Ta Move forward one sentence
.It M-a Ta Move backward one sentence
.It C-\(ua / M-\&{ Ta Move to previous paragraph
.It C-\(da / M-\&} Ta Move to next paragraph
.It M-h Ta Mark current paragraph (with its blank separator line)
.It C-Home / M-\&< Ta Move to beginning of buffer
.It C-End / M-\&> Ta Move to end of buffer
.It M-m Ta Back to indentation on current line
.It M-r Ta Cycle cursor through top/middle/bottom of window
.It M-g g / M-g M-g Ta Go to line (prompts for line or line:col)
.It M-g n / M-g M-n Ta Jump to the next match or compilation diagnostic
.It M-g p / M-g M-p Ta Jump to the previous match or compilation diagnostic
.It M-. Ta Go to the definition of the symbol at point (needs a language server)
.It M-? Ta List every reference to the symbol at point (needs a language server)
.It M-\&, Ta Go back to where the last xref jump started
.It M-TAB Ta Complete the symbol before point (needs a language server)
.It C-u Ta Numeric prefix for the next command (see below)
.El
.Pp
.Ic C-u
captures a count that the next command runs that many times.
Type
.Ic C-u
alone for 4, repeat
.Ic C-u
to multiply by 4 each time
.Pq Sq C-u C-u No \(rA 16, Sq C-u C-u C-u No \(rA 64 ,
or follow
.Ic C-u
with digits to set an explicit count
.Pq Sq C-u 8 No \(rA 8 , Sq C-u 1 0 0 No \(rA 100 .
.Ic M-0
through
.Ic M-9
also start or extend a numeric prefix directly
.Pq Sq M-1 M-\&! .
.Ic C-g
cancels an in-progress prefix.
The accumulated count is shown in the echo area while it is being built,
and is capped at 1000 repeats.
.Pp
Whether an argument was supplied and what it counts to are separate
questions, as in Emacs.
.Ic C-u 0
supplies an argument whose count is zero, so
.Ic C-u 0 C-x e
does not replay the macro at all, while a command that only asks whether
an argument is present treats zero as present, so
.Ic C-u 0 C-x C-e
still inserts its result.
A prefix typed before
.Ic C-x ,
.Ic C-x r
or
.Ic C-c
reaches the command the following key names.
.Pp
Cursor motion, character and word deletion, word-case commands, opening
lines, inserting newlines and inserting plain characters all honour the
count;
.Sq C-u 80 -
inserts eighty dashes, handy for header rules.
Commands without a meaningful repeat semantics ignore the count silently.
.Pp
.Ic M-a
and
.Ic M-e
use a deliberately simple sentence-boundary heuristic: a sentence ends at
.Sq \&. ,
.Sq \&? ,
or
.Sq \&!
immediately followed by whitespace.  Mid-text periods such as
.Dq Mr. Smith
or
.Dq e.g.\&
will be mis-detected as sentence ends.
.Ss Editing
.Bl -column "C-Home / C-End" "XXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXX" -compact
.It Sy Key Ta Sy Action
.It Enter Ta Insert newline with auto-indent
.It C-j Ta Same as Enter; in Lisp Interaction/Lisp mode evaluates the sexp before point and inserts the result
.It Tab Ta Insert tab (with bracket autocomplete)
.It Backspace Ta Delete character before point
.It Delete Ta Delete character at point
.It C-d Ta Delete character before point
.It C-k Ta Kill from point to end of line
.It M-d Ta Kill from point to the end of the next word
.It M-Backspace Ta Kill from point back to the start of the previous word
.It C-o Ta Open line (insert newline, keep cursor)
.It C-q Ta Quoted-insert: insert the next byte literally
.It C-t Ta Transpose characters around point
.It M-t Ta Transpose the two words around point
.It M-/ Ta Expand the word before point from this buffer
.It M-\e Ta Delete horizontal space around point
.It M-Space Ta Collapse horizontal space around point to one space
.It M-z Ta Kill through a prompted character
.It M-^ Ta Join current line with the previous one
.It M-u Ta Upcase word forward from point
.It M-l Ta Downcase word forward from point
.It M-c Ta Capitalize word forward from point
.It M-\&; Ta Toggle line comment
.El
.Pp
.Ic C-k
at end of line joins it with the next line (Emacs behavior).
Killed text is appended to the kill ring when
.Ic C-k
commands are consecutive.
.Pp
.Ic C-q
reads the next byte from the terminal verbatim and inserts it,
bypassing auto-indent and bracket completion.  Used to embed a
literal Tab, Escape, or other control byte — handy when editing
terminfo, sendmail.cf or similar files that need a specific byte
the regular key bindings would otherwise consume.
.Pp
.Ic M-\&;
toggles a line comment on the current line using the comment prefix defined
by the current syntax (e.g.
.Sq //
for C,
.Sq #
for Python and Shell).
If the mark is set, every line between the mark and the cursor is toggled.
When adding a comment the prefix is inserted at column 0 followed by a space;
when removing one, the prefix and the optional following space are deleted.
.Ss Copy and Paste
.Bl -column "C-Home / C-End" "XXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXX" -compact
.It Sy Key Ta Sy Action
.It C-Space Ta Set mark at point
.It C-u C-Space Ta Jump to mark, popping the mark ring
.It M-@ Ta Mark the next word, leaving point where it is
.It C-w Ta Kill region (cut to kill ring)
.It M-w Ta Copy region (copy to kill ring)
.It C-y Ta Yank from kill ring (paste)
.El
.Pp
Set the mark with
.Ic C-Space ;
the region between point and mark renders in reverse video and
tracks the cursor as it moves.
Cut it with
.Ic C-w ,
copy with
.Ic M-w ,
or paste from the kill ring anywhere with
.Ic C-y .
The highlight is per-buffer; it survives a
.Ic C-x b
switch and reappears when you come back, and it follows Emacs'
transient-mark convention:
the region deactivates on
.Ic C-g ,
on the first edit, and after a region command
.Pq Ic C-w , M-w
has consumed it.
The mark itself stays set for the next
.Ic C-x C-x .
Marks are persistent buffer positions: inserting or deleting text before
or through one relocates it with the edit, and undo relocates it through
the replayed edit as well.
The kill ring is shared across all buffers.
.Pp
.Ic M-@
marks the word after point without moving point.
Repeating it, or using it while a region is live, pushes the mark another
word along, so the region grows a word at a time;
.Ic C-u N M-@
takes N words at once.
.Pp
Each buffer also keeps a mark ring of former marks.
Setting a mark pushes the previous one onto the ring, and
.Ic C-y ,
.Ic M-<
and
.Ic M->
push a mark at point before moving, as in Emacs.
.Ic C-u C-Space
jumps to the mark and rotates the ring, so repeating it walks back
through earlier positions.
The ring holds 16 entries per buffer.
.Ss Shift Selection and CUA Clipboard
.Bl -column "C-Home / C-End" "XXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXX" -compact
.It Sy Key Ta Sy Action
.It S-\(<- / S-\(-> Ta Extend region one character
.It S-\(ua / S-\(da Ta Extend region one line
.It S-Home / S-End Ta Extend region to line bounds
.It Shift-Delete Ta Cut region
.It Shift-Insert Ta Paste from kill ring
.It Ctrl-Insert Ta Copy region
.It Delete Ta Delete active region (or character if none)
.El
.Pp
Shift+motion drops the mark at point and extends the region as the
cursor moves, so anyone coming from a modern GUI editor no longer
has to remember
.Ic C-Space
first.
The region is transient: a non-shifted key tears it down after the
command runs, while region-consuming commands
.Pq Ic C-w , M-w , C-x C-x
still see the mark intact during their dispatch.
Mixing styles works: an explicit
.Ic C-Space
mark stays sticky when extended with shift+motion.
.Pp
The CUA clipboard trio maps to the same kill ring as
.Ic C-w / M-w / C-y .
Note that some terminals (Terminator, gnome-terminal, ...) intercept
.Ic Shift-Insert
and
.Ic Ctrl-Insert
for their own clipboard handling; unbind them in the terminal
preferences if you want
.Nm
to see them.
.Ss Rectangles
.Bl -column "C-Home / C-End" "XXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXX" -compact
.It Sy Key Ta Sy Action
.It C-x Space Ta Rectangle mark mode
.It C-x r k Ta Kill rectangle (cut)
.It C-x r y Ta Yank rectangle (paste at point)
.It C-x r d Ta Delete rectangle (no save)
.It C-x r c Ta Clear rectangle (spaces)
.It C-x r t Ta Replace rectangle with a string
.El
.Pp
.Ic C-x Space
sets a rectangle mark at point and turns the highlight rectangular:
every row in the row range gets reverse-video on the same visual
column span, lined up across tabs and UTF-8 content.
The same transient-mark tear-down rules apply:
.Ic C-g ,
an edit, or a region command ends the mode.
.Pp
.Ic C-x r y
pastes the last killed rectangle at point, padding short target rows
with spaces and appending new rows when the buffer is too short.
.Ic C-x r c
overwrites every char in the rectangle with a space, padding short
rows out to the right edge so the rectangle
.Dq exists
everywhere it should.
.Ic C-x r t
prompts for a string and replaces each row's span of the rectangle
with it; with a zero-width rectangle this inserts the string in the
same column on every row.
The string is one row's worth of text: a newline quoted into it with
.Ic C-q
is refused, with the buffer, the mark and the undo history untouched,
since a rectangle replacement that split its own rows would leave
nothing for one undo step to restore.
Each rectangle command is one undo step;
.Ic C-_
restores the entire affected range, including any padding or row
appends.
.Ss Registers
.Bl -column "C-Home / C-End" "XXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXX" -compact
.It Sy Key Ta Sy Action
.It C-x r Space Ta Save point in a register
.It C-x r j Ta Move point to a register's saved position
.It C-x r s Ta Copy the region into a register
.It C-x r i Ta Insert a register's text at point
.El
.Pp
A register is named by the next key you type after the sequence: one
printable character, so
.Ic C-x r Space a
saves point in register
.Ql a
and
.Ic C-x r j a
goes back to it.
.Ic C-g
at that prompt cancels; any other key
.Pq a control key, an arrow, a non-ASCII character
is refused with
.Do
Not a register name
.Dc
and changes nothing.
There are 32 register slots.
Storing into a name that already has one replaces it; a name that has
none takes a free slot, and when every slot is in use a new name is
refused rather than one of the existing registers being discarded.
.Pp
A saved position is a persistent marker, not a line number: text
inserted or deleted before it moves it with the surrounding text, so
.Ic C-x r j
returns to the same place in the text rather than to the same line
number.
.Ic C-x r j
switches the selected window to the register's buffer when that is a
different one, exactly as switching buffers by any other means would;
kg has no window-configuration registers, so which window shows the
position is always the one you are in.
A register whose buffer has been killed reports
.Do
Register points into a buffer that is gone
.Dc
and moves nothing \(em it never jumps into whatever buffer has since
taken the freed slot.
Such a register keeps reporting that until it is stored into again.
.Pp
.Ic C-x r s
copies the region into a register and
.Ic C-x r i
inserts that register's text at point.
The bytes are stored exactly, so an insertion gives back what was
copied, byte for byte, whatever the encoding.
.Ic C-x r i
is one edit and therefore one undo step:
.Ic C-_
removes the whole insertion.
It leaves the mark where the insertion began, the way a yank does, so
.Ic C-x C-x
selects what was just inserted.
.Ic C-x r s
needs a region and refuses without one
.Pq Do No mark set Dc ;
in rectangle mark mode it refuses too, since a rectangle is not a byte
range \(em
.Ic C-x r k
and
.Ic C-x r y
are the rectangle's own commands.
.Pp
A register holds a position or text, never both: asking for the wrong
one reports
.Do
Register holds the other kind of thing
.Dc
and does nothing.
One register holds at most 1 MiB and all of them together at most
4 MiB; a copy over either limit is refused with the region, the
registers and the buffer untouched.
A numeric argument is not part of any of these four commands and is
ignored.
.Ss Search and Replace
.Bl -column "C-Home / C-End" "XXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXX" -compact
.It Sy Key Ta Sy Action
.It C-s Ta Incremental search forward
.It C-r Ta Incremental search backward
.It M-C-s Ta Incremental regexp search forward
.It M-C-r Ta Incremental regexp search backward
.It M-% / Esc % Ta Query replace
.El
.Pp
While searching:
type to extend the query;
.Ic C-s
or
.Ic \(->
or
.Ic \(da
finds the next match;
.Ic C-r
or
.Ic \(<-
or
.Ic \(ua
finds the previous match;
.Ic Enter
accepts the match;
.Ic Esc
cancels and returns to the original position;
.Ic Backspace
removes the last character from the query.
.Pp
.Ic C-q
is Emacs'
.Ic isearch-quote-char :
the next key joins the query literally, whatever it is, which is the
only way to search for a character no printable key produces.
.Ic C-q C-j
searches for a newline, so
.Ic C-s word C-q C-j
finds
.Sq word
only where it ends a line and leaves point at the start of the next
one, and
.Ic C-q Tab
searches for a literal tab.
.Pp
.Ic C-y
appends the kill ring's newest entry to the query and searches for the
result at once, as Emacs'
.Ic isearch-yank-kill
does.
.Ic M-y
directly after such a yank replaces the just-yanked text with the next
older ring entry and searches again
.Pq Emacs' Ic isearch-yank-pop-only ,
wrapping past the oldest back to the newest;
.Ic M-y
with no yank immediately before it appends the newest entry exactly as
.Ic C-y
does, which is also Emacs' behavior.
With an empty kill ring either key changes nothing and the prompt
answers
.Ic [kill ring empty]
for one keystroke.
A literal query matches across a line break: a newline in it, whether
put there by
.Ic C-q C-j
or carried in by a yanked kill, matches the break at the end of a row,
so a multi-line kill yanked with
.Ic C-y
finds the span it came from.
A regexp query is still matched a line at a time, so a pattern that
spans a break never matches; Emacs, searching the buffer as one text,
matches that too.
kg's last row has no separator after it \(em a file's final newline is
its terminator rather than a row of its own \(em so a query cannot match
past the end of the buffer.
.Pp
Searches have a history.
Accepting a search with
.Ic Enter ,
or ending it on its match with one of the movement keys above, records
the query;
.Ic Esc
and
.Ic C-g
do not.
While searching,
.Ic M-p
recalls the previous search string and searches for it again,
.Ic M-n
walks back toward the query that was being typed before the first
.Ic M-p ,
and
.Ic C-s
or
.Ic C-r
with an empty query repeats the last search, so
.Ic C-s C-s
finds the next occurrence of it.
Literal and regexp searches keep separate rings, as in Emacs.
Both kinds match the text in the buffer rather than the text as it is
drawn: a TAB is one character and never the eight columns it occupies, so
a search for eight spaces passes an indented line by, and point after a
match lands just behind the match whatever the line is indented with.
.Pp
.Ic M-%
or
.Ic Esc %
prompts for a search string and a replacement string, then steps through
every match from the current position to the end of the buffer.
When a region is active the search is confined to that region instead.
At each match:
.Ic y
or
.Ic Space
or
.Ic Enter
replaces the match and moves to the next;
.Ic n
skips the match;
.Ic \&!
replaces the current match and all remaining matches without prompting;
.Ic q ,
.Ic Esc ,
or
.Ic C-g
stops the search.
Each replacement is independently reversible with
.Ic C-_ .
.Pp
.Ic M-C-s
or
.Ic M-C-r
runs incremental regexp search.
Supported Emacs-like regular expression dialect:
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
.Ic .
matches any character except newline.
.It
.Ic ^
and
.Ic $
anchor to line boundaries.
.It
.Ic * ,
.Ic + ,
and
.Ic ?
quantifiers.
.It
.Ic \e(...\e)
for grouping.
.It
.Ic \e|
for alternation.
.It
.Ic \e{n\e} ,
.Ic \e{n,\e} ,
and
.Ic \e{n,m\e}
for intervals.
.It
Bare
.Ic ( ,
.Ic ) ,
.Ic | ,
.Ic { ,
and
.Ic }
are treated as literal characters.
.It
Character classes
.Ic [...] ,
.Ic [^...] ,
ranges, and POSIX bracket classes (e.g.
.Ic [:digit:] ,
.Ic [:alpha:] ,
.Ic [:space:] ,
.Ic [:lower:] ,
.Ic [:upper:] ).
.It
Smart-case matching (case-insensitive unless an uppercase character is present).
.El
.Pp
The matcher bounds the work one pattern may spend on one line, and a
pattern that would exceed it -- a quantified group nested inside another
quantifier, over a long run of the repeated character, say -- is reported
as such rather than answered.
Incremental search shows
.Ic [too complex]
in its prompt, and
.Ic M-x query-replace-regexp
stops there and says how many replacements it had made.
Neither means the pattern is absent: the line may well contain a match
this matcher could not reach.
.Pp
.Ic M-x query-replace-regexp
prompts for a regular expression and a replacement template.
The replacement template supports:
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
.Ic \e&
for the whole match.
.It
.Ic \e1
through
.Ic \e9
for capture groups.
.It
.Ic \e\e
for a literal backslash.
.El
.Ss Undo
.Bl -column "C-Home / C-End" "XXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXX" -compact
.It Sy Key Ta Sy Action
.It C-_ / C-/ Ta Undo last change
.El
.Pp
Undo history is per-buffer.
Each buffer keeps up to 1000 operations.
The buffer is marked unmodified when undo returns to the last-saved state,
or to the state the buffer was opened at.
Beyond 1000 operations the oldest ones are discarded, and the saved state
goes out of reach with them: the buffer then stays modified until it is
saved again.
.Ss File and Buffer Commands
.Bl -column "C-Home / C-End" "XXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXX" -compact
.It Sy Key Ta Sy Action
.It C-x C-s Ta Save current buffer
.It C-x s Ta Save all modified buffers
.It C-x C-x Ta Exchange point and mark
.It C-x C-w Ta Write buffer to a different file (save as)
.It C-x i Ta Insert file contents at point
.It C-x C-f Ta Open file in new buffer
.It C-x C-r Ta Open file read-only in new buffer
.It C-x C-q Ta Toggle read-only mode on current buffer (read-only-mode)
.It C-x d Ta List a directory (dired)
.It C-x b Ta Switch to buffer, creating it when the name is new
.It C-x k Ta Kill (close) current buffer
.It C-x C-b Ta Open buffer list
.It C-x C-c Ta Quit
.It C-x C-e Ta Eval-last-sexp; with a prefix arg, insert the result into the buffer
.It C-x # Ta Save and exit with status 0 (EDITOR-server done)
.El
.Pp
The filename prompts for
.Ic C-x C-f ,
.Ic C-x C-r ,
.Ic C-x C-w
and
.Ic C-x i
open an ido-style picker.
Matching directory entries render as a {pipe-delimited} pick list in
the echo area, with the currently selected entry shown in bold.
.Pp
What you type narrows the list by substring, the way Emacs'
ido-mode does it: typing
.Sq buf
matches both
.Sq bufmgr.c
and
.Sq editor_buffer.c .
Prefix matches still rank above mid-name ones, so a leading letter
prioritises the obvious candidate.
Files already open in a buffer get pushed to the back of the list,
matched by basename the way Emacs does it, so the default
selection lands on something not yet open.
Handy when walking a directory to open several files in turn.
.Pp
Left/Right arrows cycle the selection;
.Ic C-b
and
.Ic C-f
move the minibuffer cursor (including at the end of the typed path), so
editing and completion selection remain distinct;
.Ic Enter
on a directory descends into it;
.Ic Enter
on a file completes the typed path and submits.
.Ic M-Enter
submits the typed text literally, applying no completion and descending
into nothing, which is how a directory is named without descending into
it; this binding is a deliberate deviation from Emacs, which has none.
.Ic Enter
does the same when the last component of the typed text is
.Sq \&.
or
.Sq \&.. ,
which is Emacs' own behavior and makes
.Ic C-x C-f \&. Enter
list the prompt's directory.
.Ic Backspace
at a trailing
.Sq /
walks you up one level in a single keystroke.
.Ic Tab
extends the typed text to the longest common prefix of the prefix-
matched group, or appends a
.Sq /
when the sole match is a directory.
.Pp
.Ic C-x b
opens the same picker shape against the active buffers (everything
except the current one).
What you type narrows the list by basename;
Left/Right cycles;
.Ic Enter
switches to the selected buffer.
A name that narrows the list to nothing is not a mistake: as in Emacs,
.Ic Enter
then creates an empty buffer with that name, whatever the name is
spelled like.
Such a buffer visits no file \(em it has a name, not a path \(em so
.Ic C-x C-s
asks where to write it,
.Ic C-x s
never offers it, and
.Ic C-x k
kills it without asking about unsaved changes.
.Ss Window Commands
.Bl -column "C-Home / C-End" "XXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXX" -compact
.It Sy Key Ta Sy Action
.It C-x 2 Ta Split window horizontally
.It C-x 3 Ta Split window vertically
.It C-x o Ta Switch to next window
.It C-x 0 Ta Delete current window
.It C-x 1 Ta Delete all other windows
.El
.Ss Keyboard Macros
.Bl -column "C-Home / C-End" "XXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXX" -compact
.It Sy Key Ta Sy Action
.It C-x ( / F3 Ta Start recording a keyboard macro
.It C-x ) / F4 Ta Stop recording
.It C-x e / F4 Ta Execute (replay) last macro
.El
.Pp
While recording,
.Ic C-x )
or
.Ic F4
stops the macro.
When not recording,
.Ic F4
(or
.Ic C-x e )
replays it; a numeric argument replays it that many times, e.g.
.Ic C-u 2 C-x e ,
and
.Ic C-u 0 C-x e
replays it zero times.
Every keystroke during recording is captured, including characters typed into
prompts (search strings, goto-line numbers, etc.), so the macro replays
the full interaction faithfully.
The macro buffer holds up to 1024 keystrokes.
.Ss Utility
.Bl -column "C-Home / C-End" "XXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXX" -compact
.It Sy Key Ta Sy Action
.It C-g Ta Cancel current operation
.It C-l Ta Recenter: cycle current line to center, top, then bottom of window
.It C-h Ta Open *help* buffer (q closes)
.It C-z Ta Suspend kg to the shell; the job resumes with fg
.It M-x Ta Execute named command
.It M-: Ta Evaluate Lisp expression
.It M-\&! Ta Run shell command; C-u prefix inserts output at point
.It M-\&| Ta Pipe region through shell command; C-u prefix replaces it with the output
.El
.Pp
.Ic M-\&!
prompts for a shell command line and runs it with
.Ql /bin/sh -c .
Without a prefix argument the command's output is shown: a single short
line goes straight to the echo area (with the exit status appended if it
was nonzero or the command died from a signal); anything longer goes to a
read-only
.Pa *Shell Command Output*
buffer, displayed in another window without disturbing the buffer or
window you were editing.
With a prefix argument
.Po
.Ic C-u M-\&!
.Pc
the command's standard output is inserted at the cursor position instead.
The command's standard error is discarded so it cannot disturb the editor.
.Pp
.Ic M-\&|
extracts the active region and prompts for a shell command.
Without a prefix argument the command runs with the region piped to
standard input and its output is displayed as with plain
.Ic M-\&! ;
the region is left untouched.
With a prefix argument
.Po
.Ic C-u M-\&|
.Pc
the region is instead replaced with the command's standard output.
The original region text is left in the kill ring so
.Ic C-y
recovers it if the result was not what was wanted.
.Pp
Both commands are intended for non-interactive filters such as
.Xr sort 1 ,
.Xr fmt 1 ,
.Xr column 1 ,
.Xr jq 1
or
.Xr sed 1 ;
interactive programs that require a terminal will not work.
.Pp
The command runs in a process group of its own, as a compilation does, so
anything it starts in the background belongs to that group and is
signalled with it rather than surviving it.
kg waits for the command to finish and does not draw or accept input while
it runs; there is no key that interrupts one, so a command that never exits
has to be dealt with from outside the editor.
.Pp
Both commands share one command-line history.
.Pp
Minibuffer prompts keep a history of what has been entered at them:
.Ic M-p
.Pq or Ic Up No or Ic C-p
recalls the previous entry and
.Ic M-n
.Pq or Ic Down No or Ic C-n
returns toward the entry being typed before recall started.
The shell-command, compile, Eval, goto-line and string-rectangle prompts
each have a ring of their own; the search-string and replacement prompts
of both
.Ic query-replace
and
.Ic query-replace-regexp
share a single one, as in Emacs.
Nothing is written to disk: the rings live only for the session.
.Pp
Prompts edit by word as well as by character.
.Ic M-f
and
.Ic M-b
move over the word after and before the cursor,
.Ic M-u ,
.Ic M-l
and
.Ic M-c
upcase, downcase and capitalize the word after it and leave the cursor
past that word, and
.Ic C-q
inserts the next byte literally.
A prompt's word is delimited by whitespace, where Emacs' is delimited by
word syntax: in a filename prompt kg's
.Ic M-b
walks back over the whole path where Emacs' stops at each
.Sq / .
.Pp
A prompt's kills and yanks use the one global kill ring, as Emacs'
minibuffer does:
.Ic C-k
kills from the cursor to the end of the typed text,
.Ic M-d
and
.Ic M-Backspace
kill the word after and before the cursor, and what was killed in a
prompt can be yanked in the buffer after the prompt closes, or the
other way around.
Consecutive prompt kills grow one ring entry, under the same
directly-following rule buffer kills use, and the prompt's last kill
never coalesces with a buffer kill after it.
.Ic C-y
inserts the newest entry at the cursor and
.Ic M-y
directly after a yank replaces it with the next older one, wrapping;
run with no yank immediately before it, the prompt answers
.Ic [Previous command was not a yank]
where Emacs 28 and later would open its interactive ring browser, which
kg does not have.
An empty kill ring answers
.Ic [Kill ring is empty]
after the typed text for one keystroke.
A yanked entry stops at an embedded NUL byte, the prompt being a single
NUL-terminated line where Emacs' minibuffer is a real buffer.
.Pp
.Ic C-h
opens a special
.Pa *help*
buffer with the full key-binding table.
It is a regular buffer.
Scroll with the usual motion keys, then press
.Ic q
to dismiss it (or
.Ic C-x k ,
or
.Ic C-x b
back to wherever you were).
.Pp
.Ic M-x
prompts for a command name and executes it.
The completion prompt is the same ido-style picker as
.Ic C-x C-f :
substring matching, Left/Right to cycle,
.Ic Tab
to extend to the longest common prefix.
Pressing
.Ic RET
with nothing typed re-runs the last command executed via
.Ic M-x ,
shown as a
.Ql (default ...)
hint in the prompt; a prefix argument given to this
.Ic M-x
invocation applies to the repeated command.
Available commands:
.Bl -tag -width "what-cursor-position"
.It auto-revert-mode
Toggle automatic re-reading of the current buffer's file when it changes
on disk.
A buffer is only auto-reverted while it is unmodified; a buffer with
unsaved edits is never silently overwritten.
.It back-to-indentation
Move point to the first non-blank character of the line.
Bound to
.Ic M-m .
.It backward-char
Move point one character back.
Bound to
.Ic C-b
and the left arrow.
A numeric argument repeats it.
.It backward-kill-word
Kill the word before point.
Bound to
.Ic M-Backspace .
A numeric argument repeats it.
.It backward-paragraph
Move point to the previous paragraph boundary.
Bound to
.Ic M-\&{
and
.Ic C-\(ua .
A numeric argument repeats it.
.It backward-sentence
Move point to the start of the sentence.
Bound to
.Ic M-a .
A numeric argument repeats it.
.It backward-word
Move point one word back.
Bound to
.Ic M-b
and
.Ic C-\(<- .
A numeric argument repeats it.
.It beginning-of-buffer
Move point to the start of the buffer, leaving the mark behind.
Bound to
.Ic M-\&<
and
.Ic C-Home .
.It capitalize-word
Capitalize the word forward from point (first letter upper, rest lower).
Equivalent to
.Ic M-c .
.It clear-rectangle
Blank out the rectangle between point and the mark, keeping its
width.
Bound to
.Ic C-x r c .
.It comment-dwim
Comment the line or region out, or uncomment it, using the mode's
comment syntax.
Bound to
.Ic M-\&; .
.It compile
Prompt for a compile command (prefilled with the current buffer's
.Va compile-command ) ,
run it via
.Ic /bin/sh -c
in the source file's directory, show combined stdout and stderr in a
read-only
.Pa *compilation*
buffer, and store the confirmed command back into the buffer.
An empty confirmed command reports
.Do
No compile command
.Dc .
.It completion-at-point
Ask the buffer's language server what the symbol before point could be,
and complete it.
Bound to
.Ic M-TAB ,
which is what a terminal sends for
.Ic C-M-i
as well.
One candidate is inserted; several that share more text than has been
typed extend it by what they share and report
.Do
Complete, but not unique
.Dc ;
several with nothing left in common are listed in a read-only
.Pa *Completions*
buffer, leaving point where it was.
An empty answer reports
.Do
No completions
.Dc .
See
.Sx LSP .
.It dabbrev-expand
Expand the word before point into a longer word this buffer already
holds, searching backward from point first, nearest occurrence first,
then forward past the word point is in.
A second
.Ic M-/
straight after the first replaces the expansion with the next candidate
rather than expanding again, and each distinct word is offered once
however often it occurs.
When the candidates run out, what was typed is put back and
.Do
No further dynamic expansion for `word' found
.Dc
is reported; when there were none to begin with, so is
.Do
No dynamic expansion for `word' found
.Dc .
Any other key ends the cycle and the last expansion stands.
Reached through
.Ic M-x
it always expands afresh rather than continuing a cycle, since a command
invoked through another one does not count as the command that ran last.
A word here is kg's own \(em ASCII alphanumerics and
.Ic _ ,
the same policy
.Ic M-f
and
.Ic M-d
use \(em rather than the major mode's syntax table, and matching is
exact-case: unlike Emacs' dabbrev, kg neither folds case nor refits the
expansion to the case pattern that was typed, and it does not continue
into other buffers.
Bound to
.Ic M-/ .
.It dap-breakpoint-temporary
Set a breakpoint on the current line that is removed the first time it is
hit, and on the end of the session either way.
Works from a read-only buffer, like every other debugger command.
See
.Sx DEBUGGER .
.It dap-breakpoint-toggle
Set a breakpoint on the current line, or clear the one that is there.
Breakpoints belong to the editor, not to a session: they can be set with
no program running, they survive the session that verified them, and a
session starting is an occasion to tell an adapter about them.
A buffer visiting no file on disk is refused, since there is no source
for an adapter to name.
See
.Sx DEBUGGER .
.It dap-continue
Let the program run until it stops again.
See
.Sx DEBUGGER .
.It dap-debug
Start a debug session.
Reads the nearest
.Pa .kg-dap.json ,
asks which of its configurations to run when there is more than one,
runs that configuration's optional build step and launches only if it
succeeded, and remembers the choice so a second
.Ic dap-debug ,
and
.Ic dap-restart ,
need not ask again.
A configuration with no program of its own \(em kg's built-in
.Ic lldb-dap
one \(em asks for the program to debug rather than guessing at one.
Refused in a buffer that visits no file: the configuration file is looked
for beside that file and
.Ic ${file}
expands to it, so there is nothing to debug and nowhere to look.
Cancelling either prompt, or naming a configuration the file does not
have, launches nothing and leaves the remembered choice alone.
See
.Sx DEBUGGER .
.It dap-disconnect
End the debug session and leave the program running.
Detaching is the point of this one; use
.Ic dap-terminate
to end the program with it.
See
.Sx DEBUGGER .
.It dap-evaluate
Read an expression and evaluate it in the selected stack frame,
reporting the answer in the echo area.
A failed evaluation is reported in place and does not end the session.
See
.Sx DEBUGGER .
.It dap-frame-down
Select the frame the selected one called, walking back towards where the
program is actually stopped.
See
.Sx DEBUGGER .
.It dap-frame-up
Select the caller of the selected frame.
The first walk of a stop is what fetches the stack: a stop itself asks
the adapter for one frame, since that is all it needs to show where the
program is.
See
.Sx DEBUGGER .
.It dap-goto
Jump execution to the current line without running what lies between.
Needs an adapter that advertises
.Ic gotoTargets ;
one that does not is not asked.
When the adapter reports several targets on the line, they are retained
and a second
.Ic dap-goto
asks which to jump to.
See
.Sx DEBUGGER .
.It dap-info-delete-breakpoint
Delete the breakpoint named by the line point is on, in one of the
debugger's panes.
Bound to
.Ic d
there.
See
.Sx DEBUGGER .
.It dap-info-select
Act on the debugger line point is on, dispatching on what that line is: a
frame is selected and its source visited, a thread line reports which
thread kg is looking at, a breakpoint is visited, and a variable is
expanded or collapsed.
Switching threads is not implemented, and the line says so rather than
claiming to have switched.
Bound to
.Ic RET
in the debugger's panes.
See
.Sx DEBUGGER .
.It dap-info-toggle-breakpoint
Enable or disable the breakpoint named by the line point is on.
A disabled breakpoint is one kg keeps and the adapter is never told
about.
Bound to
.Ic D
in the debugger's panes.
See
.Sx DEBUGGER .
.It dap-info-toggle-breakpoints-threads
Swap the breakpoints and threads panes, which share one slot of the
debugger's layout.
Bound to
.Ic t
in the debugger's panes.
See
.Sx DEBUGGER .
.It dap-many-windows
Toggle the debugger's window layout: the REPL and the locals across the
top, the source and the program's output in the middle, the stack and the
breakpoints below.
The window arrangement in force when it is turned on is restored when it
is turned off, and when the session ends.
Bound to
.Ic F12
while a session is running.
See
.Sx DEBUGGER .
.It dap-next
Step over the call on the current line.
See
.Sx DEBUGGER .
.It dap-pause
Ask a running program to stop.
See
.Sx DEBUGGER .
.It dap-repl
Evaluate an expression in the debugger's REPL.
Shows
.Pa *dap-repl*
in another window, prompts with
.Ql "DAP> " ,
echoes the expression into the transcript and appends the answer beside
it when the adapter replies \(em replies can finish out of order, so each
one names the expression it answers.
The input is the minibuffer because the transcript is read-only.
See
.Sx DEBUGGER .
.It dap-restart
Restart the program under the same configuration, keeping the breakpoint
table and the window layout.
Adapters that advertise the protocol's own
.Ic restart
request are asked for it; the rest are ended and started again.
See
.Sx DEBUGGER .
.It dap-step-in
Step into the call on the current line.
See
.Sx DEBUGGER .
.It dap-step-instruction
Step one instruction rather than one line.
Adapters that do not advertise stepping granularity are asked for an
ordinary
.Ic dap-next
instead of for a request they might refuse.
See
.Sx DEBUGGER .
.It dap-step-out
Run until the selected frame returns.
See
.Sx DEBUGGER .
.It dap-terminate
End the debug session and the program with it.
See
.Sx DEBUGGER .
.It dap-until
Run to the current line and stop there.
A one-shot breakpoint is set first and the program is let go only once
the adapter has confirmed it \(em so a
.Ic dap-until
whose line the adapter refuses stops nothing and says so, rather than
running to the end.
A breakpoint you already had on that line is not disturbed: only the
one-shot half ends at the stop, and the breakpoint keeps its condition.
See
.Sx DEBUGGER .
.It delete-backward-char
Delete the character before point.
Bound to
.Ic Backspace .
A numeric argument repeats it.
.It delete-char
Delete the character after point.
Bound to
.Ic C-d .
A numeric argument repeats it.
.It delete-forward-char
Delete the active region if there is one, otherwise the character
after point.
Bound to
.Ic Delete .
.It delete-horizontal-space
Delete spaces and tabs around point on the current line.
Equivalent to
.Ic M-\e .
Reversible with
