.It lisp-arena-stats
Report the state of the fixed Lisp arena in the status area: total object
slots, free slots, the high-water mark of live objects, the number of
collections, the peak GC root-stack depth, the peak evaluator frame depth
against the arena's frame capacity, the number of allocations that
failed, the arena's own size in bytes, and last the payload region \(em
bytes live of bytes carved, their high-water mark, compactions, and
payload requests that failed.
The payload region is a part of the arena kg does not currently ask for,
so every one of those five numbers is zero.
It allocates nothing and changes nothing, so asking cannot alter the
answer, and it is the way to see whether a session that reported
.Ql out of memory
recovered.
That last use has one wrinkle, and it follows from the first: slots come
back only when a collection runs, and once a session is under way a
collection runs only when an allocation finds the free list empty.
Asking is not an allocation, so straight after an exhaustion the command
reports
.Ql 0 free
even where the data is already unreachable.
Evaluate anything at all, then ask again, to see what came back.
A session that has only just started already reports one collection
rather than none: kg forces a single one at the end of Lisp
initialisation, so that what loading the prelude left unreachable is
reclaimed before any of the session's own work begins.
Also callable from Lisp as
.Ql (command-execute "lisp-arena-stats") .
