.Sh BUFFERS
.Nm
supports up to 32 open buffers.
Each buffer has its own undo history, dirty flag, and read-only state.
Special system buffers (the buffer list with
.Ic C-x C-b ,
the help buffer with
.Ic C-h )
have names enclosed in
.Sq *
and can be closed with
.Ic q
when another buffer is available to switch to.
.Pp
The kill ring is global and shared across all buffers, so text killed in
one buffer can be yanked in another; the minibuffer prompts and the
incremental-search query share it too, in both directions
(see
.Sx KEY BINDINGS Ns 's
prompt and search paragraphs).
It holds up to 16 entries and 8 MiB total; pushing past either cap
evicts the oldest entries first, and an individual entry too big to
ever fit alone is rejected outright, leaving the ring as it was.
.Ic C-y
always yanks the newest entry;
.Ic M-y
.Pq yank-pop ,
run immediately afterward, walks older ones.
A kill replaces what was there, but a kill immediately following
another kill grows that entry instead, regardless of which kill
command either one was
.Pq C-k , M-d , M-Backspace , M-z and C-w
all count, as long as nothing else \(em another command, a prompt,
.Ic C-g ,
a buffer switch \(em ran in between.
A forward kill
.Pq C-k , M-d , M-z , and C-w when point had moved past the mark
appends to the end of the entry; a backward kill
.Pq M-Backspace , and C-w when point had moved back before the mark
prepends to its start, so two backward kills in a row still read in
buffer order.
A copy
.Pq M-w
always starts a fresh entry, and so does any command that is not
itself a kill.
A yank or yank-pop is its own kind of entry point again: only
.Ic M-y ,
run with nothing else in between, may follow one.
.Sh COMPILATION
.Ic M-x compile
prompts for a command line, prefilled with the current buffer's
.Va compile-command
.Pq default Ql make -k ,
and runs it with
.Ic /bin/sh -c
in the directory containing the source file.
For unsaved or special buffers the working directory is the current
directory
.Pq Fn getcwd .
.Pp
The confirmed command is stored back into the buffer's
.Va compile-command
so
.Ic M-x recompile
can repeat it without prompting.
An empty confirmed command reports
.Do
No compile command
.Dc .
.Pp
.Ic M-x recompile
re-runs the last compile command.
From an ordinary buffer it uses that buffer's
.Va compile-command ;
from
.Pa *compilation*
it reuses the last executed command and working directory.
.Pp
Output is captured asynchronously and streams incrementally into the
.Pa *compilation*
buffer, with combined stdout and stderr.
While compilation is running, editing and window management remain responsive.
The active compilation process can be terminated with
.Ic M-x kill-compilation
or
.Ic C-c C-k
inside the
.Pa *compilation*
buffer.
Retained output is capped at 8\~MiB.
Every kept byte counts against that cap, line terminators and the still
incomplete last line included, so neither a flood of blank lines nor one
endless line can grow the buffer past it.
Truncation is noted in the buffer; the process keeps running, its remaining
output is still read and discarded, and its exit status is still reported.
.Pp
Compilation is permitted while the source buffer is read-only.
.Pp
Every
.Ql file:line[:column]:
diagnostic in the output
.Pq and, for GNU make, the directory a relative path is read against
is recorded as it streams in, up to 128 per run; further diagnostics are
counted but not kept, and the buffer says so.
.Ic M-x next-error
.Pq Ic M-g n
and
.Ic M-x previous-error
.Pq Ic M-g p
step through them, opening or selecting the named file, in another window
when
.Pa *compilation*
is the selected one and a second window exists, otherwise in the current
window, and moving point to the recorded position.
The two commands share one cursor through the list, owned by them and not
by the recorded diagnostics; running either past either end reports
.Do
No more diagnostics
.Dc
or
.Do
No previous diagnostics
.Dc
and stays there rather than wrapping.
A numeric argument moves that many diagnostics at once.
.Pp
Revisiting a diagnostic after editing its file returns to the same place
in the text even if the edit moved it, because the position is tracked as
the file changes rather than re-read from the diagnostic's original line
number; a file whose buffer was killed since the last visit, or that
cannot be opened at all, loses that tracking and reports the problem
instead of guessing.
Starting a new compilation
.Pq including Ic recompile
discards the previous run's diagnostics and cursor, so
.Ic next-error
starts over from the first one.
.Sh OCCUR
.Ic M-x occur
prompts
.Do
List lines matching regexp:
.Dc
and lists every line of the current buffer matching that regular
expression in a read-only
.Pa *Occur*
buffer.
An empty answer cancels.
The pattern is matched case-insensitively unless it contains an
upper-case letter, the same rule the search commands use.
There is no default key binding, as in Emacs.
.Pp
The listing opens in another window while the source stays visible, and
point stays in the source: reading the answer does not cost the place the
question was asked from.
Its first line reports the counts, the pattern and the buffer searched
\(em for example
.Do
3 matches in 2 lines for \(dqfoo\(dq in buffer: kg.c
.Dc
\(em and each line after it is a matching line of the source, its number
in a seven-column field, a colon, and the line's own text.
A line holding several matches appears once, with each match highlighted
where it sits.
Up to 500 matches are listed; the listing says so when it stops there.
.Pp
Inside
.Pa *Occur* ,
.Ic Enter
.Pq Ic occur-mode-goto-occurrence
visits the occurrence the row names \(em in the other window when there
is one, so the listing stays on screen \(em
.Ic n
and
.Ic p
move between rows, and
.Ic q
closes the listing.
.Pp
Running
.Ic occur
also makes it the
.Ic next-error
store, so
.Ic M-g n
and
.Ic M-g p
walk the matches from anywhere, in the order they appear in the buffer; a
line with several matches is one row and several stops.
Running a compilation takes the two keys back, and running
.Ic occur
again takes them from a compilation.
.Pp
The rule is the same in every direction and worth stating once: the
listing
.Em created
last owns
.Ic M-g n ,
whether it came from
.Ic occur ,
from a compilation, or from
.Ic M-x lsp-diagnostics .
Emacs is last\-wins too, but its
.Dq last
is a different thing \(em
.Va next-error-last-buffer
is the listing last
.Em navigated ,
and failing that the current buffer.
So sitting in
.Pa *compilation*
after opening a diagnostics listing and never moving in it,
.Ic M-g n
walks the diagnostics in kg and the compilation in Emacs.
With one listing at a time, which is the usual case, the two rules agree.
.Pp
Each match is remembered as a position in the source
.Em buffer ,
not as a line of a file: a buffer that was never saved is listed and
visited like any other, and a match visited after the source was edited
lands where its text moved to rather than where it used to be.
A match whose tracking was lost falls back to the line and column it was
found at, clamped to what the buffer now holds.
.Pp
Rerunning
.Ic occur
replaces the listing and restarts the
.Ic next-error
cursor at the first match.
A search that finds nothing reports
.Do
No matches for \(dq...\(dq
.Dc
and changes nothing at all: the previous listing, its highlights and its
walk are all still there.
.Sh LSP
kg can ask a Language Server Protocol server where a symbol is defined,
where it is used, what it could be completed to, and to rename it.
Five commands use it:
.Ic M-x xref-find-definitions ,
bound to
.Ic M-. ,
.Ic M-x xref-find-references ,
bound to
.Ic M-\&? ,
.Ic M-x xref-go-back ,
bound to
.Ic M-\&, ,
.Ic M-x completion-at-point ,
bound to
.Ic M-TAB ,
and
.Ic M-x lsp-rename ,
which is bound to nothing.
It is optional at build time
.Pq Ic make WITH_LSP=0 ,
on by default, and
.Ic kg -V
prints
.Sq +lsp
or
.Sq -lsp
to say which this binary is.
.Pp
Servers are found at run time and started lazily.
Nothing is spawned by opening a file: the first command that needs an
answer is what pays for the server, and every buffer under the same
workspace root then shares it.
Five modes have a built-in server:
C
.Pq Ic clangd ,
Python
.Pq Ic ty server ,
Go
.Pq Ic gopls ,
Rust
.Pq Ic rust-analyzer
and Java
.Pq Ic nbcode .
Any other mode reports that it has no server rather than starting one.
.Pp
The workspace root is the nearest ancestor directory holding a marker of
that language's build system \(em for C
.Pa .clangd ,
.Pa compile_commands.json ,
.Pa compile_flags.txt
or
.Pa build/compile_commands.json ;
for Python
.Pa ty.toml ,
or a
.Pa pyproject.toml
mentioning
.Ql [tool.ty ;
for Go
.Pa go.mod
or
.Pa go.work ;
for Rust
.Pa Cargo.toml ;
for Java
.Pa pom.xml ,
.Pa build.gradle ,
.Pa build.gradle.kts ,
.Pa settings.gradle
or
.Pa settings.gradle.kts
\(em then the nearest ancestor holding
.Pa .git ,
and failing that the file's own directory.
.Pp
The nearest marker wins, which for Go and Rust is the answer their own
tooling gives.
A file inside a module roots on that module even when a
.Pa go.work
sits above it, because the go command finds the workspace above the module
on its own; a file in a Cargo workspace member roots on the member, because
.Ic cargo metadata
resolves the workspace above it.
Java's markers are the files a Java server's
own Maven and Gradle importers look for, and there the rule is a compromise
in one place: a Gradle subproject's
.Pa build.gradle
is nearer than the
.Pa settings.gradle
at the top of the build it belongs to, so
.Nm
starts a server per subproject and a definition in a sibling subproject is
not in that server's model.
An Eclipse
.Pa .project
file is deliberately not a marker: it is per-project state that an imported
build has in every subdirectory, and the build files above it are the truth.
.Pp
.Ic M-.
sends the question and returns; the editor stays responsive while the
server thinks, and point moves when the answer arrives.
The echo area reports what happened: the file and line jumped to, or
.Do
No definition found
.Dc ,
or the reason there was no server to ask.
Before the jump, the place it left is pushed on the mark ring, so
.Ic C-u C-SPC
returns to it.
.Pp
.Ic M-\&,
.Pq Ic M-x xref-go-back
walks that back: it returns to where the newest jump started and forgets
it, so pressing it repeatedly retraces the route \(em out of the visited
file to the
.Pa *xref*
listing, and out of that to where the search was started.
Sixteen departure points are kept, the oldest dropped; each is a marker,
so it follows the text if the file is edited in between, and one whose
buffer has been killed is passed over rather than reported.
With nothing left to return to it says
.Do
No xref history
.Dc .
.Pp
.Ic M-\&?
answers with a list rather than a jump, in a read-only buffer named
.Pa *xref* :
a header line counting the results, then one line per result in
.Ql path:line:column: preview
form, with the path relative to the workspace root when it is under it.
The preview is the text of the line the result is on, with leading and
trailing blanks dropped and its length capped at 200 bytes; a control
byte in it becomes a space, so one result is always exactly one line of
the listing.
It is read from the buffer visiting that file when there is one, so an
unsaved edit is what the listing shows; otherwise it is read from the
file, one line at a time and no further than a mebibyte in.
A file that cannot be read, a line past the end of one, and a blank line
all get no preview, and the result is listed without one.
No file is opened into a buffer to paint a listing.
The listing is bounded at 200 results and says how many more there were
when it cut.
.Ic M-.
uses the same listing when a server offers more than one definition; a
single definition is still visited directly.
.Pp
In the
.Pa *xref*
buffer:
.Bl -tag -width "Enter" -compact
.It Ic Enter
Go to the result on this line
.Pq Ic M-x xref-goto-xref .
.It Ic n , Ic p
Next and previous line.
.It Ic q
Close the listing.
.El
.Pp
The column a listing prints is the protocol's own character offset
counted from one, which is a byte column on every line that is ASCII;
.Ic Enter
converts it against the target line's real bytes, so the place it lands
is right whether or not the printed number was.
An answer that arrives while a minibuffer prompt is open is built but not
selected \(em the echo area says how many results are waiting in
.Pa *xref* ,
and
.Ic C-x b
reaches them.
That holds for a single definition too: an answer never moves point or
switches buffers under a prompt, so
.Ic M-.
lists rather than jumps when one arrives while a prompt is up.
.Pp
.Ic M-x lsp-rename
renames the symbol at point everywhere the server says it appears.
It prompts for the new name \(em the prompt spells the old one, and an
empty answer means it \(em sends
.Ql textDocument/rename ,
and applies the WorkspaceEdit that comes back, in either of the two
shapes a server may send it in.
Every occurrence in one buffer is applied as a single replacement of the
span they lie in, so the whole rename is one undo record: one
.Ic C-_
takes all of it back, in that buffer.
A file that no buffer visits is opened, edited and left modified and
unsaved \(em Emacs' behaviour, and what lets the change be read before it
is written \(em and the buffer the command was run in is selected again
when it is done.
The report is
.Do
Renamed N occurrences in M files
.Dc .
.Pp
A rename is all or nothing, across every file it names.
The whole answer is resolved and checked before one byte is written \(em
every file opens, every buffer is writable, every range is inside the
file it names, no two edits overlap \(em and a single failure refuses the
rename entirely, names the file and the reason, and leaves every buffer
as it was, including one that was opened only to check it.
A rename that renamed most of the uses of a symbol is worse than one that
renamed none and said so.
.Pp
An answer that is no longer about the buffer is refused the same way.
kg sends a document to a server only when a command needs it, so between
the question and the answer the buffer can move \(em one character typed
while the server is thinking is enough \(em and the ranges then name text
that is not where it was.
Such an answer is refused with
.Do
\*[Lt]file\*[Gt] changed while the server was answering
.Dc ,
and a server that sends versioned
.Ql documentChanges
is refused the same way when the version is not the one kg last sent it,
which is the check eglot makes.
.Pp
Two other things a rename does not do.
It does not perform the create-, rename- and delete-file operations a
server may attach to its answer: they are counted, and the report names
how many were skipped and of what kind.
And it applies nothing at all when part of the answer cannot be read \(em
an edit naming a file that is not a local one, or more of them than kg
stores.
.Pp
.Ic M-TAB
.Pq Ic M-x completion-at-point
completes the symbol before point.
What is being completed is the range the server itself named, when it
sent one, and otherwise the word before point \(em the same word
.Ic M-/
expands.
Candidates are filtered to those that match what has been typed, since a
server may return its whole set, and ordered by the
.Ql sortText
the protocol asks a client to sort on.
Then, exactly as in Emacs: one candidate is inserted; several sharing
more than has been typed insert what they share and report
.Do
Complete, but not unique
.Dc ;
several with nothing left in common are listed in a read-only
.Pa *Completions*
buffer beside the source, with point left where it was.
An empty answer says
.Do
No completions
.Dc .
The insertion is one edit, so
.Ic C-_
takes a completion back in one step.
A completion whose buffer changed while the server was answering is
refused rather than inserted over text that is no longer there, the same
staleness check a rename makes.
.Pp
The
.Pa *Completions*
listing closes itself once point leaves the symbol it is a listing of,
which is Emacs' rule and includes Emacs' surprising half: typing more of
that symbol leaves the listing up, unrefreshed, while a space, a
.Ic C-a
back past where the completion began, or a move to another line takes it
down.
Stepping into it with
.Ic C-x o
keeps it, so it does not vanish as the reader arrives; repeating
.Ic M-TAB
keeps it and replaces it when the next answer lands.
It is passive in the one way it was: unlike Emacs', it binds nothing.
It is an ordinary read-only buffer \(em
.Ic C-x 0
closes its window by hand, closing it does not kill it, and the next
completion rebuilds it in place.
.Pp
The buffer is sent to the server before each request, not on every
keystroke: an unsaved buffer is still the text the answer is about, and a
server never sees an edit that no command asked a question about.
Killing a buffer tells every server holding it that the document is
closed, and quitting
.Nm
shuts every running server down.
A server that dies is not restarted where it fell; the next command
starts a new one.
That is worth saying twice for a server that fails to start at all:
every retry is a whole new process, so four
.Ic M-.
after a server that dies on startup are four servers started and four
failures reported, which for a JVM-backed one is four JVMs.
The message says what happened each time; nothing is retried behind your
back and nothing is remembered but the failure.
.Pp
Servers go away with
.Nm .
A clean exit \(em
.Ic C-x C-c ,
or any of the other ways out \(em asks every running server to
.Sq shutdown
and then to
.Sq exit ,
and kills whatever has not gone by the end of the grace period.
A
.Nm
that is killed outright, with
.Dv SIGKILL
or by the terminal going away, has no chance to do any of that: a server
on the ordinary wire notices anyway, since its standard input ends when
kg's descriptors are closed, but one started over
.Sq listen-hash:
speaks over a socket and need never read its standard input, so it can
outlive the editor and has to be stopped by hand.
.Pp
Every request carries a deadline, since a server that is alive but stuck
answers nothing and only death is otherwise noticed.
Thirty seconds after it was sent, an unanswered request is abandoned: the
echo area reports it once, naming the method and the wait
.Pq Sq no reply to textDocument/definition after 30s ,
the same line is written to
.Pa *lsp-log* ,
and nothing else happens \(em the server is left running, a reply that
arrives afterwards is dropped, and the next command asks it again.
.Ev KG_LSP_TIMEOUT_MS
overrides the thirty seconds, in milliseconds, and
.Sq 0
switches the deadline off for a session debugging a server by hand.
.Pp
.Pa *lsp-log*
is where a server's own words go: everything it writes to its standard
error, one line at a time and prefixed with the server's name, together
with every request that ran out of time and the reason a server died.
It is created on the first line and never selects itself or takes a
window \(em
.Ic C-x b Pa *lsp-log*
is how it is read \(em and it keeps the last 64 KiB, dropping whole lines
from the top.
.Pp
Positions are exchanged in whichever encoding the handshake settles on.
.Nm
offers UTF-8 first, because its own columns are byte offsets and nothing
has to be converted;
.Ic clangd
and
.Ic ty
both take it.
A server that insists on the protocol's default, UTF-16, gets UTF-16:
columns are converted to and from code units against the real bytes of
the line, so a line with non-ASCII text before point is asked about, and
answered at, the right place either way.
.Pp
Diagnostics are the one part of the protocol
.Nm
never asks for: a server publishes them whenever it has an opinion about
a document it has been told about, and
.Ic M-x lsp-diagnostics
lists what has arrived.
A publish replaces everything that server had said about that file
rather than adding to it, which is the protocol's own rule, and one
carrying a version older than the last one seen for that file is dropped
whole \(em it describes text the server has since been sent a newer copy
of.
The listing is a read-only buffer named
.Pa *Diagnostics* ,
shown in another window and not selected, with one row per diagnostic in
.Ql file:line:column: severity: message
form, ordered by file and then by position.
.Ic Enter
visits the one on the current line,
.Ic n
and
.Ic p
move,
.Ic q
closes, and showing the listing takes the
.Ic M-g M-n
and
.Ic M-g M-p
keys, so next-error walks the diagnostics until another command produces
results.
.Pp
The command sends the current buffer to its server before listing.
That is not what Emacs' flymake does, and it is what makes diagnostics
reachable at all here:
.Nm
opens a document lazily, on the first command that needs one, so a
session in which no LSP command had been run would have nothing to list
forever.
The first
.Ic M-x lsp-diagnostics
in a buffer therefore starts the server and asks; the answer arrives
afterwards, like every other answer.
.Pp
Every diagnostic is also marked in any buffer visiting its file, as a
temporary highlight over the range the server named, repainted on every
publish and taken back when a publish says there is nothing left.
Severity picks the highlight's priority, so an error covers a warning
where the two overlap, but not its colour: the renderer's colour channel
is one foreground number and the warning face is already the red an
error wants.
The severity is spelled out in the listing, which is where it is
legible.
.Pp
.Ic M-x lsp-hover
asks the server what the symbol at point is.
.Nm
has no eldoc, so nothing asks by itself and there is no key binding \(em
Emacs has none either, since eldoc is automatic there.
The answer's contents are read in all three shapes the protocol has had
(a MarkupContent object, a bare string, and a MarkedString or an array of
them) and rendered to plain text: Markdown is neutralised rather than
displayed, so fences, backticks, heading markers and horizontal rules go
and what is left is the words.
The first line goes to the echo area; an answer with more lines in it
also goes whole to
.Pa *lsp-hover* ,
which the message then names and which
.Ic C-x b
reaches.
That buffer is created lazily and never selects itself, exactly as
.Pa *lsp-log*
does not.
Emacs' eglot shows the same first line and offers the rest through
.Ic M-x eldoc-doc-buffer ,
on demand;
.Nm
writes the rest unconditionally and shows it never.
.Pp
.Ev KG_LSP_SERVER_C ,
.Ev KG_LSP_SERVER_PYTHON ,
.Ev KG_LSP_SERVER_GO ,
.Ev KG_LSP_SERVER_RUST
and
.Ev KG_LSP_SERVER_JAVA
replace the built-in command line for that mode.
The value is run through
.Ic /bin/sh -c ,
exactly as
.Ic M-x compile Ns 's
command is, so a wrapper script, an absolute path with spaces in it and
extra arguments all work.
.Pp
A value beginning with the token
.Sq listen-hash:
and then whitespace runs the rest of itself as the command and speaks to
it over a TCP socket rather than over its standard input and output:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
KG_LSP_SERVER_JAVA='listen-hash: nbcode \e
    --start-java-language-server=listen-hash:0'
.Ed
.Pp
That is Oracle's nbcode, the NetBeans Java server, which speaks no LSP on
stdio at all.
Started that way it listens on a port, prints
.Sq Java Language Server listening at port N with hash H
on its standard output, and expects the client to connect to
.Li 127.0.0.1:N
and write the hash before the first LSP byte.
.Nm
reads its standard output for that line, connects without blocking, sends
the hash, and frames travel over the socket both ways; everything else the
server prints there, that line included, joins its standard error in
.Pa *lsp-log* .
Java's own built-in row uses that wire, since
.Ic nbcode
is the Java default; the token is what an override needs when it points at
another one.
An override otherwise speaks stdio whatever the row it replaces uses, so
.Ev KG_LSP_SERVER_JAVA Ns = Ns Ic jdtls
is jdt.ls on its own arrangement: the wire belongs to the command, and an
override that inherited nbcode's would write a handshake at a server with
nothing to answer it with.
.Pp
Java's server is Oracle's
.Ic nbcode ,
the NetBeans-based server behind the Java extension for VS Code
.Pq Ic oracle/javavscode ,
spawned as the bare name
.Ic nbcode
with both
.Fl -start-java-language-server Ns = Ns Ic listen-hash:0
and
.Fl -start-java-debug-adapter-server Ns = Ns Ic listen-hash:0 .
It is the default because it is also the Java debugger: one
.Ic nbcode
process announces a language server and a Java debug adapter on one
standard output, so debugging Java talks to the server that is already
open rather than starting a second toolchain.
.Pp
.Nm
sends it one thing it sends no other server: an
.Ic initializationOptions.nbcodeCapabilities
object, which turns its Java support on and names the command namespace it
answers under.
Every flag in it for a facility
.Nm
does not have \(em a status bar, a test-results view, an HTML page
renderer \(em is sent false, since advertising a facility it lacks is how
a client is sent messages it silently drops.
.Pp
The Eclipse JDT Language Server was the Java default before
.Ic nbcode
and is one override away
.Pq Ev KG_LSP_SERVER_JAVA Ns = Ns Ic jdtls ,
though Java cannot be debugged through it: the Java debug adapter is a
socket the
.Ic nbcode
process announces beside its language server.
It ships as a tarball rather than a package, and
.Pa utils/install-jdtls.sh
in the source distribution downloads the newest milestone, or builds a
checkout given
.Fl -from-source ,
and installs a launcher into
.Fl -prefix ,
.Pa ~/.local
by default.
It needs Java 21 or newer, which is what
.Ic jdtls
itself refuses to start below, and a
.Ic python3
to run jdt.ls's own launcher script.
A JVM starting and importing a project is slower than a
.Ic clangd
parsing one translation unit: expect the first
.Ic M-.
in a Java buffer to take a few seconds.
.Pp
.Ic nbcode
does not speak LSP on stdio at all.
Started with a
.Ic listen-hash:0
server option it prints a port and a 128-character hash per server on its
standard output and waits to be connected to; the client opens a TCP
socket to that port on
.Ic 127.0.0.1
and writes the hash before the first LSP byte.
.Pp
.Pa utils/install-nbcode.sh
in the source distribution installs it, taking the same options as the
jdtls script.
By default it downloads the published extension from open-vsx.org \(em a
zip with a complete NetBeans runtime in it \(em and unpacks that runtime
into
.Pa <prefix>/share/nbcode ;
.Fl -from-source
builds a javavscode checkout with
.Ic ant
instead, which wants that checkout's
.Pa netbeans
submodule fetched and
.Ic ant apply-patches
already run.
Java 17 or newer either way.
.Pp
The
.Pa <prefix>/bin/nbcode
wrapper it writes gives every run a userdir of its own.
NetBeans' single-instance handler is keyed on the userdir, so two
.Ic nbcode
processes pointed at one do not both start: the second hands its command
line to the first and exits without printing a port, which from the
outside is a server that started and then said nothing.
.Ev KG_NBCODE_USERDIR
pins one instead, keeping its index warm between sessions at the price of
running one
.Ic nbcode
at a time.
.Sh DEBUGGER
kg speaks the Debug Adapter Protocol, so a program can be run under a
debug adapter from inside the editor: breakpoints in the source, a
stopped line, stepping, an expression evaluated in the frame the program
is stopped in, and a window layout that shows the rest.
It is optional at build time
.Pq Ic make WITH_DAP=0 ,
on by default, and
.Ic kg -V
prints
.Sq +dap
or
.Sq -dap
to say which this binary is.
A binary built without it still has every
.Ic dap-*
command, and each of them answers
.Do
kg was built without DAP support
.Dc
rather than leaving
.Ic M-x
to report a command nobody has heard of.
.Pp
Adapters are found on
.Ev PATH
at run time and nothing is downloaded or installed, the same rule the
language-server client follows.
Four are built in:
.Ic lldb-dap
for C and C++,
.Ic python3 -m debugpy.adapter
for Python,
.Ic dlv dap
for Go \(em see
.Sx GO DEBUGGING
below \(em and
.Ic nbcode-java
for Java, which is not a command at all, see
.Sx JAVA DEBUGGING .
kg's shipped debugpy configuration sets
.Li "console": "internalConsole"
and
.Li "subProcess": false ,
which is what routes the program's output through the protocol rather
than through a terminal kg does not have.
.Pp
An adapter kg starts, and the program that adapter runs, get no
controlling terminal: kg's own belongs to kg.
The program's output therefore arrives as protocol output events and is
shown in
.Pa *dap-output* ,
and a program that wants a terminal of its own \(em one that reads
keystrokes, or draws its own screen \(em is not something this version
can run under the debugger.
Attaching to it as it runs elsewhere is the way to debug such a program.
.Pp
.Ss Keys
The default table is gud's, so that an Emacs user's fingers already know
it.
.Bl -tag -width "PageUp / PageDown" -compact
.It Ic F9 No / Ic C-F9
.Ic dap-breakpoint-toggle
and
.Ic dap-breakpoint-temporary
.It Ic F4
.Ic dap-evaluate
.It Ic F5 No / Ic C-F5
.Ic dap-step-in
and
.Ic dap-step-instruction
.It Ic F6 No / Ic F7
.Ic dap-next
and
.Ic dap-step-out
.It Ic F8
.Ic dap-continue
.It Ic F10 No / Ic M-F10
.Ic dap-until
and
.Ic dap-goto
.It Ic F11 No / Ic M-F11
.Ic dap-restart
and
.Ic dap-terminate
.It Ic F12
.Ic dap-many-windows
.It Ic M-up No / Ic M-down
.Ic dap-frame-up
and
.Ic dap-frame-down
.It Ic PageUp No / Ic PageDown
the same two, while a session is running
.El
.Pp
They live in two keymaps rather than one, because the two halves of a
debugger become available at different times.
.Ic dap-breakpoint
holds
.Ic F9
and
.Ic C-F9
and is live in any buffer that visits a file, with nothing running \(em
setting a breakpoint before starting is the milestone's own first step.
.Ic dap
holds everything else and is live exactly while a session exists, so
outside one those keys are still the user's own.
.Ic M-x dap-debug
is reachable whatever the keys say.
.Pp
Both are ordinary native keymaps, so an init file may rebind them:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
(define-key 'dap-mode-map "<f5>" 'dap-continue)
(define-key 'dap-breakpoint-mode-map "<f9>" 'dap-breakpoint-toggle)
.Ed
.Pp
.Ic global-set-key
stays
.Ic C-c Ns -only by design; F-keys are reachable through
.Ic define-key
precisely because these are real native maps.
.Pp
Every command is callable from Lisp as
.Ql (command-execute "dap-continue")
and the like; the four that prompt \(em
.Ic dap-debug ,
.Ic dap-evaluate ,
.Ic dap-goto
and
.Ic dap-repl
\(em are refused from a Lisp activation that has no terminal to prompt
on, rather than reading from a descriptor that is not there.
None of them edits buffer text, so all of them work in a read-only
buffer: debugging somebody else's source is the ordinary case.
.Ss The panes
.Ic F12
arranges six windows:
.Pa *dap-repl*
and
.Pa *dap-locals*
across the top,
the source and
.Pa *dap-output*
in the middle, and
.Pa *dap-stack*
and
.Pa *dap-breakpoints*
below.
A second
.Ic F12
puts the previous window arrangement back and forgets it, so a later one
saves the arrangement in force then rather than resurrecting the first.
The end of a session puts it back too, but only while the layout is on
screen.
A terminal too small for six windows refuses cleanly and keeps the
arrangement it has, and a terminal that has since shrunk too far for the
saved arrangement refuses the way back the same way: the layout stays up,
the snapshot is kept, and the next
.Ic F12
with room restores it.
Windows split or deleted by hand while the layout is up are discarded
with it.
A pane whose name is already a buffer of your own is not taken over: the
layout says whose buffer it is and does not go up.
.Pp
.Pa *dap-breakpoints*
and
.Pa *dap-threads*
share the lower-right slot, as
.Xr gdb 1 Ns 's
do;
.Ic dap-info-toggle-breakpoints-threads Pq Ic t No in a pane
swaps them.
.Pp
In any of the six,
.Ic RET
acts on what the line IS: a frame is selected and its source visited, a
thread line reports which thread kg is looking at \(em there is no thread
switch yet, and the line says so \(em a breakpoint is visited, and a
variable is expanded or collapsed.
.Ic d
deletes the breakpoint on the line,
.Ic D
enables or disables it \(em a disabled breakpoint is one kg keeps and the
adapter is never told about, since
.Ic setBreakpoints
replaces a source's whole set \(em and
.Ic q
buries the pane.
A line built during an earlier stop is refused rather than acted on:
every adapter measured answers a stale frame or variable handle with
success and silently wrong data.
.Pp
.Pa *dap-output*
and
.Pa *dap-repl*
are transcripts rather than listings: append-only, bounded at four
mebibytes each, with one visible marker where they cut, and draining
continues past the cap so that an adapter can never block writing to kg.
Killing one starts a fresh transcript that says so \(em kg keeps no second
copy.
The debuggee's bytes are untrusted and reach the screen only through the
same visible spelling every other byte in kg does, so an escape sequence
in a program's output is displayed rather than obeyed.
Lines of
.Ic stderr
are marked with a leading
.Ql "! " ,
and no ANSI is ever inserted.
.Pp
The other four panes are rebuilt from the tables on every change, about a
tenth of a second after it, and only while they are on screen.
.Ss Breakpoints
A breakpoint is a place in a file, and it belongs to the editor.
It exists with no adapter running, it survives the session that verified
it, and a session starting is an occasion to tell an adapter about the
table rather than an occasion to own it.
In an open buffer a breakpoint is anchored to the text, so an edit above
it takes it with the line it was set on; in a file nobody has open it is
remembered as a line number and re-anchors when the file is opened.
.Pp
Breakpoints are marked in the first column of their line: one colour for
a breakpoint an adapter has verified and another, dimmer one for a
breakpoint no adapter has confirmed \(em because none is running, or
because the one that is running could not place it.
The line the program is stopped on is marked across its width.
All three are projections of what kg knows; they are rebuilt whenever a
buffer is opened and when the session ends, and they are never a second
copy of the table.
.Pp
An adapter may move a breakpoint \(em debugpy silently relocates a line
past the end of a function and calls it verified \(em and what is marked
is the line the adapter reported, not the line that was asked for.
.Ss Launch configurations
.Pa .kg-dap.json ,
in the nearest ancestor directory of the file being debugged, says what
to run.
That file's directory is what
.Li ${workspaceRoot}
means.
With no such file anywhere above, kg's built-in configurations apply and
.Li ${workspaceRoot}
is the file's own directory.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
{ "version": 1,
  "configurations": [
    { "name": "Python current file",
      "adapter": "debugpy",
      "request": "launch",
      "build": { "command": "make", "cwd": "${workspaceRoot}" },
      "arguments": { "program": "${file}",
                     "cwd": "${workspaceRoot}" } } ] }
.Ed
.Pp
.Li adapter
is a built-in adapter's name or an inline object with
.Li command ,
.Li args ,
.Li transport
and
.Li cwd
for one kg does not ship.
.Li request
is
.Li launch
or
.Li attach .
.Li build
is optional; when it is there,
.Ic dap-debug
runs it through kg's ordinary compilation machinery \(em the same
.Pa *compilation*
buffer, the same diagnostics, the same
.Ic C-c C-k
\(em and launches only if it exited zero.
.Li arguments
is passed to the adapter untouched: the members kg does not know are the
point, since an adapter's launch arguments are its own vocabulary.
.Pp
Five substitutions are made inside
.Li arguments
strings:
.Li ${file} ,
.Li ${fileDir} ,
.Li ${fileUri} ,
.Li ${workspaceRoot}
and
.Li ${env:NAME} .
.Li ${fileUri}
is
.Li ${file}
as a
.Li file:
URI, percent-encoded, for the one adapter that parses that argument as a
URI rather than as a path; a relative buffer name is made absolute against
kg's working directory first, since a URI cannot be relative.
The set is closed, and an unknown one is an error rather than an empty
string \(em
.Li ${workspaecRoot}/prog
expanding to
.Pa /prog
is a debugger that silently debugs the wrong program.
Substitution happens inside decoded string values and is never a textual
splice into the JSON, so a path containing a quote or a backslash arrives
as that path.
.Pp
.Ss JAVA DEBUGGING
.Nm
debugs Java through the language server it is already talking to.
One
.Ic nbcode
process announces a Java language server and a Java debug adapter on one
standard output, and the built-in
.Ic nbcode-java
adapter is the second of those, so
.Ic M-x dap-debug
in a
.Pa .java
buffer starts, or reuses, the same
.Ic nbcode
that
.Ic M-.
uses.
There is no command to spawn and no port to configure: the address and its
handshake come from the language server.
.Pp
The ordering is a requirement rather than a preference.
The debug connection captures the language session's state when it is
made, so
.Nm
will not connect before that session has finished initializing; a slow
start names the step it is on in the echo area, and
.Ic M-x dap-disconnect
stops waiting.
.Pp
The built-in
.Li "Java (nbcode)"
configuration debugs the file under the cursor with no project setup:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
{ "name": "Java (nbcode)",
  "adapter": "nbcode-java",
  "request": "launch",
  "arguments": { "type": "jdk", "file": "${fileUri}",
                 "classPaths": ["any"],
                 "console": "internalConsole" } }
.Ed
.Pp
An inline adapter asks for the same wire with
.Li "transport": "lsp-sibling"
and an
.Li lspLanguage .
.Pp
Ending a Java session leaves
.Ic nbcode
running: it is the user's language server, and
.Ic dap-disconnect
closes one socket and nothing else, so sequential debug sessions share the
one process.
.Ic nbcode
also never sends the protocol's
.Li terminated
event, so
.Nm
infers the end of a run from the debuggee's threads going away and
disconnects after a short grace period during which late output is still
collected; a new thread or a stop cancels the inference.
.Pp
With
.Ev KG_LSP_SERVER_JAVA
pointing at a server that is not
.Ic nbcode ,
there is no debug adapter to reach and
.Ic dap-debug
says so rather than starting something.
.Ss GO DEBUGGING
.Ic M-x dap-debug
in a
.Pa .go
buffer offers the built-in
.Li "Go (delve)"
configuration, which debugs the package the file is in:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
{ "name": "Go (delve)",
  "adapter": "delve",
  "request": "launch",
  "arguments": { "mode": "debug", "program": "${fileDir}",
                 "cwd": "${fileDir}", "stopOnEntry": false } }
.Ed
.Pp
.Li "mode": "debug"
has delve build the package itself and debug what it built, which is why
.Li program
is a directory rather than a binary; delve passes
.Li -gcflags="all=-N -l"
on that path, so the locals are readable without anybody asking.
.Ic dlv
and the Go toolchain must be on
.Ev PATH ;
.Ev KG_DAP_ADAPTER_DELVE
replaces the command line for a delve installed somewhere else.
.Pp
.Li "mode": "exec"
debugs a binary that is already built, and
.Li program
is then that binary.
Build it with
.Bd -literal -offset indent
go build -gcflags="all=-N -l" -o prog .
.Ed
.Pp
or its locals will be missing or unreadable, and the only thing that says
so is the scope's own name, which
.Nm
shows verbatim:
.Li "Locals (warning: optimized function)" .
.Li mode
also takes
.Li test ,
.Li replay
and
.Li core .
.Pp
Three things about a Go session differ from the other adapters.
.Pp
The program's own output arrives on delve's
.Em standard error
rather than as protocol output events, so
.Nm
routes that channel into
.Pa *dap-output*
as raw bytes: no newline is invented at a read boundary, and a program
that printed CRLF or stopped mid-line looks like it did.
.Pp
A failed build lands in
.Pa *compilation* .
The launch response says only that the debug console has the details; the
real
.Li ./main.go:8:14: undefined: ...
arrived earlier, so
.Nm
keeps launch-phase output and, when the launch is refused, feeds it
through the ordinary compilation machinery \(em
.Ic M-g n
walks the errors as it does any compiler's.
A compilation of the user's own that is already running keeps that buffer,
and the output stays in
.Pa *dap-output* .
.Pp
delve runs where the configuration says, not where
.Nm
does.
It resolves
.Li program
and writes its
.Pa __debug_bin
artefact relative to its own working directory, so the built-in
.Li delve
adapter runs in
.Li ${workspaceRoot} .
An inline adapter's
.Li cwd
overrides that; it is the
.Em adapter's
directory and is unrelated to
.Li arguments.cwd ,
which is the program's.
.Nm
never deletes delve's build artefacts \(em they are delve's to clean up.
.Pp
An inline adapter asks for the same wire with
.Li "transport": "spawn-port"
and an
.Li announcePrefix ,
the exact text the port follows on the adapter's standard output.
It must include the host
.Pq Li "DAP server listening at: 127.0.0.1:" ,
because a port is read only after it and
.Nm
connects only to loopback: a log line must never be able to redirect the
editor to another address.
.Ss THE CONFIGURATION FILE IS TRUSTED
.Sy This file is trusted code .
A launch configuration names a command to spawn and arguments handed to a
debugger that will run a program; there is nothing to sandbox and no
pretence of one, the same story as
.Pa init.el .
What the validation is for is the other failure: a mistyped file that
would otherwise start the wrong thing without saying so.
Unknown
.Li version ,
duplicate configuration names, duplicate JSON keys, an invalid
.Li request ,
an unknown transport and a NUL inside a path are all refused with a
message naming the file and the byte the parser stopped at.
Every collection is bounded: 256 KiB of file, 64 configurations, 32
arguments to an adapter command.
.Ss Stopping and stepping
Stepping needs a stopped thread, and there is one answer to whether there
is one: without it every execution command reports
.Do
No stopped thread: the program is not suspended
.Dc
and nothing is sent.
.Pp
When the program stops, kg asks for the thread list, then for one stack
frame, and shows that frame's source if the adapter named a file that is
there.
A frame naming a path that is relative, that does not exist, or that the
adapter can only produce on request is listed but not opened \(em the
first stack of a C program routinely contains all three \(em and kg then
fetches a page of frames and shows the topmost one it can open.
When it can open none, source focus stays where it was and kg says so.
.Pp
Line numbers are the protocol's, counted from one.
Columns are ignored for navigation: an adapter's column is a position in
its own notion of the file, not a byte offset into kg's row
.Pq see Pa doc/coordinates.md .
.Ss Ending a session
.Ic dap-disconnect
leaves the program running,
.Ic dap-terminate
ends it, and quitting kg terminates a program kg launched but only
detaches from one it attached to.
An adapter that cannot be asked politely is closed, and a child kg
started is killed as the backstop.
.Sh READ-ONLY MODE
.Ic M-x read-only-mode
.Pq bound to Ic C-x C-q
toggles the current buffer between read-only and writable.
.Ic M-x toggle-read-only
is a compatibility alias.
.Pp
The read-only state is buffer-local and survives buffer and window
switches.
A read-only buffer rejects every editing command, showing
.Do
Buffer is read-only
.Dc
in the status area.
The verdict is the command's, not the key's: the same command is refused
whether it is reached by its key, by
.Ic M-x ,
by a
.Ic C-c
binding or from Lisp.
.Pp
Opening a file with
.Fl R
or
.Ic C-x C-r
initially opens it read-only;
.Ic C-x C-q
can subsequently make such a buffer writable.
.Pp
A buffer visiting a file you cannot write also comes up read-only, as in
Emacs, so the edit is refused at the first keystroke rather than at the
save that would have failed.
A file carrying no write permission at all is treated that way even for
a privileged user who could write it regardless.
A name that does not exist yet inherits its directory's answer, so a new
file in a directory you may write is an ordinary writable buffer.
The verdict is taken when the file is visited and when it is reverted,
and is not revisited afterwards: a file that becomes writable later
leaves the buffer read-only until
.Ic C-x C-q
says otherwise, which is also how the state is overridden in the first
place.
A file-local
.Va buffer-read-only
setting is applied after the verdict and replaces it, in either
direction.
.Pp
The mode line shows an
.Do
RO
.Dc
indicator when the buffer is read-only.
.Sh LOCAL VARIABLES
.Nm
supports a limited subset of Emacs file-local variables, parsed
without evaluation.
Two variables are recognized:
.Bl -tag -width "compile-command"
A numeric argument zaps through that many occurrences.
.It Va compile-command
A quoted string such as
.Ql "make all && ./a.out" .
.It Va buffer-read-only
Either
.Ql t
or
.Ql nil .
.El
.Pp
Variables are set through two mechanisms:
.Bl -enum
.It
First-line
.Fl *- ... No -*- Sy modeline
.Pq or second-line after a Li #! shebang line .
Example:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
/* -*- compile-command: "make all && ./a.out"; -*- */
.Ed
.It
End-of-file
.Sy "Local Variables:"
\&...
.Sy End:
block, searched within the final 3000 bytes of the file
.Po honoring a final form feed Pc .
The comment prefix and suffix are derived from the
.Sy "Local Variables:"
line.
Example:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# Local Variables:
# compile-command: "make test"
# buffer-read-only: t
# End:
.Ed
.El
.Pp
String values may span multiple lines with backslash-newline continuation:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# Local Variables:
# compile-command: "cc foo.c \e
#   -o foo"
# End:
.Ed
.Pp
Precedence: footer overrides modeline; file-local overrides
directory-local.
.Pp
.Ar eval
is ignored; unknown variables are ignored; merely visiting a file
never runs
.Va compile-command .
Execution happens only through an explicit
.Ic M-x compile
or
.Ic M-x recompile .
.Sh DIRECTORY-LOCAL VARIABLES
.Nm
implements a safe, deliberately limited subset of
.Pa .dir-locals.el .
A file named
.Pa .dir-locals.el
is searched for upward from the visited file's directory to the
filesystem root; the nearest match wins.
.Pp
Only the generic
.Ql nil
selector is applied.
Supported variables are
.Va compile-command
and
.Va buffer-read-only ;
all other entries
.Pq including Ql eval , Ql mode , Ql subdirs ,
.Ql auto-mode-alist , mode-specific selectors, vectors, and backquote
are ignored without evaluation.
The optional leading
.Sq '
quote is accepted.
.Pp
Example:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
((nil . ((compile-command . "make all")
         (buffer-read-only . t))))
.Ed
.Pp
Defensive limits: 64\~KiB file, 64-deep nesting, 4096 tokens.
File-local variables override directory-local.
.Pp
.Em No Emacs Lisp is evaluated
from
.Pa .dir-locals.el .
.Sh EXTERNAL CHANGES
.Nm
records each open file's device, inode, size and nanosecond timestamps as
last seen.
A background poll catches files that have been rewritten, replaced or
removed by another process, and tags the affected buffer with a
.Sq (changed)
marker in the mode line.
A file that cannot be examined at all — an unreadable directory, an I/O
error, a symlink loop — is tagged the same way: unable to check is never
reported as unchanged.
A file that was never there — a new file visited with
.Ic C-x C-f
and not yet written — is recorded as absent, which is a state like any
other: its first save asks nothing, and only something appearing at that
name in the meantime makes it a change.
On
.Ic C-x C-s
against such a buffer the user is prompted to confirm before overwriting,
either
.Sq File ... changed on disk.  Save anyway?
or, when the file cannot be examined at all,
.Sq Cannot verify ... on disk.  Save anyway? .
Both default to no.
.Ic C-x s
asks the same question for each buffer it offers to save.
.Pp
.Ic C-x C-w
asks a different question about a different file: whenever the name typed
there already exists and is not the file the buffer already holds, it
prompts
.Sq File ... exists.  Overwrite?
before the buffer adopts the name.
Declining leaves the buffer, its name and its recorded file state exactly
as they were.
.Pp
A save writes to a temporary file beside the target, flushes it, checks one
last time that the target is still the file the user agreed to replace, and
only then renames it into place and flushes the directory entry.
If the target changed in between, nothing is written and the buffer reports
.Sq ... changed on disk during the save; nothing written .
Saving through a symbolic link replaces the file the link points at, not
the link.
.Pp
With
.Ic auto-revert-mode
(or its global counterpart
.Ic global-auto-revert-mode )
turned on, a clean buffer whose file has changed on disk is silently
reloaded so the view always matches the file.
A file that has been removed, or that cannot be examined, is only marked,
never reverted: the buffer is then the last copy of its contents.
Modified buffers are never reverted automatically — the
.Sq (changed)
marker stays visible until either the user saves (and is prompted) or
explicitly runs
.Ic M-x revert-buffer
to discard local edits.
.Sh WINDOWS
The terminal can be divided into multiple windows with
.Ic C-x 2
(horizontal split) or
.Ic C-x 3
(vertical split).
Each window shows one buffer and maintains its own scroll position and cursor.
Multiple windows may show the same buffer.
Use
.Ic C-x o
to cycle between windows.
Up to 8 windows may be open simultaneously.
.Pp
Closing a window with
.Ic C-x 0
or
.Ic C-x 1
hands its point back to the buffer it was showing, so displaying that
buffer again resumes where the closed window was reading rather than at
the top.
.Pp
The smallest terminal
.Nm
lays out for is 3 rows by 8 columns: one text row, one mode line and the
echo area.
A smaller terminal is treated as that size, so the display overflows but
the editor keeps working; a reported size larger than 10000 in either
direction is rejected as implausible and the previous size is kept.
.Sh MOUSE
.Nm
asks the terminal for SGR-encoded mouse reports
.Pq DECSET 1000 , 1002 No and 1006
whenever it enters raw mode, and takes the request back whenever it
leaves: on exit, and on
.Ic C-z ,
which hands the mouse back to the shell and asks for it again on resume.
.Pp
There is no capability query worth having \(em a terminal that does not
understand the request ignores it and no harm is done \(em so the
heuristic is deliberately generous:
.Nm
asks unless
.Ev TERM
is unset, empty,
.Ql dumb
or
.Ql unknown .
.Pp
What the reports do:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Left press
Select the window under the pointer, the way
.Ic C-x o
does, and put point at the clicked position.
The terminal column is a display column, so a click lands on the
character drawn there whatever tabs and double-width glyphs precede it;
a click past the end of a line lands at the end of that line.
.It Left drag
The press position becomes the mark the first time the pointer moves \(em
a plain click leaves the mark where it was \(em and point follows the
pointer, so the region grows as an ordinary
.Ic C-SPC
region does and
.Ic C-w
or
.Ic M-w
takes it.
A drag that wanders out of the window it started in keeps extending the
region in that window.
.It Wheel
Scroll the window under the pointer five lines a notch, matching Emacs'
terminal default.
Point keeps its place in the buffer until the window scrolls past it, and
is then dragged to the edge it left.
.El
.Pp
A press on a mode line or on the echo area does nothing, and neither do
the middle and right buttons.
.Pp
Mouse reporting takes the terminal's own selection away: while it is on,
dragging selects a
.Nm
region instead of marking text for the terminal's copy buffer.
Hold
.Ic Shift
while dragging to get the terminal's native selection back for one drag;
terminals reserve Shift for exactly this.
.Pp
.Ic M-x xterm-mouse-mode
toggles the whole feature off and on again, for a terminal that answers
badly or for getting the native selection back for longer than one drag.
It is also callable from Lisp, so
.Li (command-execute \(dqxterm-mouse-mode\(dq)
in the init file turns it off for good.
While it is off, a mouse report that arrives anyway is still consumed
whole and discarded: no part of one ever reaches a buffer as text.
.Sh CONTROL CHARACTERS
Nothing
.Nm
reads becomes something the terminal obeys.
Bytes from a file, a filename, a directory listing, a Lisp string or a
subprocess' output are always drawn as characters, never as terminal
control sequences, whatever mode or syntax the buffer is in.
.Pp
A control character is shown in caret notation:
.Ql ^@
through
.Ql ^_
for the bytes below a space, and
.Ql ^?
for
.Ql DEL .
The C1 controls
.Pq U+0080 No through U+009F ,
which an eight-bit terminal reads as
.Ql CSI ,
.Ql OSC
and
.Ql DCS
introducers, and bytes that are not valid UTF-8 at all, are shown as
.Ql \e Ns Ar xnn
with the byte's value in lowercase hexadecimal, one spelling per byte.
.Pp
These spellings occupy the columns they are drawn in, so the cursor, the
mode line's column indicator, tab stops and rectangles all count a
.Ql ^[
as two columns and a
.Ql \ex9b
as four.
Tabs are not control characters here: they expand to the next tab stop as
usual.
.Sh SHOW PAREN
With point immediately after a closing bracket, or on an opening one,
.Nm
colours that bracket and the one it pairs with.
This is on by default, as
.Xr emacs 1 Ns 's
.Ic show-paren-mode
has been since 28.1;
.Ic M-x show-paren-mode
turns it off and on again.
.Pp
.Ql () ,
.Ql []
and
.Ql {}
all pair.
Nesting is counted across all three kinds together and the kind is checked
only once the partner has been found, so an opening round bracket closed by
a square one is a found pair reported as a mismatch, in a second colour.
A bracket with no partner at all gets that same mismatch colour, on its own.
.Pp
Which bracket at point is taken follows
.Xr emacs 1
exactly: the closer before point wins over an opener after it, and point
just
.Em inside
a bracket highlights nothing.
.Pp
A bracket counts only if the syntax scanner puts it in the same class \(em
code, string, or comment \(em as the bracket at point.
So brackets inside one string pair with each other, a bracket inside a
comment never closes live code, and code skips a bracket that is quoted or
commented out.
In a buffer
.Nm
has no syntax for, every byte is code and the quotes around a bracket mean
nothing; that is plain-text behaviour and is the one place this is
knowingly looser than
.Xr emacs 1 .
The other divergence runs the other way: where the bracket at point is
inside a comment and its partner would be outside it,
.Xr emacs 1
sometimes pairs the two and
.Nm
always reports no partner.
.Pp
The search stops after 102400 bytes \(em
.Xr emacs 1 Ns 's
.Va blink-matching-paren-distance ,
and for the same reason: a keystroke may not cost a scan of the whole file.
A search that runs that far reports no partner, which is also what
.Xr emacs 1
does.
.Sh SYNTAX HIGHLIGHTING
Syntax highlighting is detected automatically from the file extension or
type, e.g., Makefile.  Hash-bang lines
.Pq #!
are used as a fallback when the extension is not recognised.
.Bl -column "JavaScript" ".js .mjs .cjs" -compact
.It Sy Language Ta Sy Extensions
.It C/C++ Ta .c .h .cpp .hpp .cc
.It Python Ta .py .pyw .pyi .pyx
.It Shell Ta .sh .bash .zsh .profile .bashrc .zshrc
.It JavaScript Ta .js .mjs .cjs
.It TypeScript Ta .ts .tsx .d.ts
.It Rust Ta .rs .rlib
.It Go Ta .go
.It Java Ta .java
.It C# Ta .cs .csx
.It PHP Ta .php .phtml
.It Ruby Ta .rb .rbw .rake
.It Swift Ta .swift
.It SQL Ta .sql .ddl .dml
.It Dart Ta .dart
.It HTML Ta .html .htm .xhtml
.It JSX Ta .jsx
.It Vue Ta .vue
.It Angular Ta .component.ts .service.ts .module.ts .directive.ts .pipe.ts
.It Svelte Ta .svelte
.It Makefile Ta Makefile .mk .mak
.It Markdown Ta .md .markdown .mkd
.It YAML Ta .yaml .yml
.El
.Ss Git Commit Mode
.Nm
recognizes
.Pa COMMIT_EDITMSG ,
.Pa MERGE_MSG ,
.Pa SQUASH_MSG ,
.Pa TAG_EDITMSG ,
.Pa NOTES_EDITMSG
and
.Pa EDIT_DESCRIPTION
by filename and switches into a dedicated mode, shown as
.Dq Git commit
in the mode line.
As with the rebase todo, the match is on the exact basename only, so
.Pa .git/COMMIT_EDITMSG
switches the mode while a file whose name merely contains the string
does not; the mode binds keys that quit the editor.
Lines starting with
.Sq #
are dimmed as comments, and characters past column 50 on the subject
line (the first non-blank, non-comment line) are shown in red as a
length warning.
.Pp
.Ic C-c C-c
saves the buffer and exits
.Nm
with status 0, letting git proceed with the commit.
.Ic C-c C-k
prompts for confirmation, then exits without saving and with a
non-zero status, so git cancels the commit.
These two bindings are built in and only active in commit buffers; they
take priority over any user Lisp binding for the same keys.
.Ic M-q
leaves the subject line and comment paragraphs untouched; body
paragraphs still reflow to
.Li fill-column
as usual.
.Pp
.Ic C-x #
saves the current buffer and exits with status 0 in
.Em any
buffer, not just commit buffers.
It serves the broader
.Ev EDITOR Ns / Ns Ev GIT_EDITOR
server flow shared by
.Xr crontab 1 ,
.Xr sudoedit 8 ,
.Xr hg 1
and similar tools.
.Ss Git Rebase Mode
A file named exactly
.Pa git-rebase-todo
(the todo list of
.Ic git rebase -i ,
found at
.Pa .git/rebase-merge/git-rebase-todo )
switches into a dedicated mode, shown as
.Dq Git rebase
in the mode line.
The match is on the exact basename only, so files that merely contain
the string in their name are unaffected.
Action words
.Pq Ic pick , reword , edit , squash , fixup , drop , exec , break , label , reset , merge , update-ref
and their single-letter abbreviations are highlighted as keywords, the
commit hash after them as a constant, an
.Ic exec
command body as a string, and comment lines are dimmed.
An unrecognized action word is shown in warning color, since a typo
there makes git fail the whole rebase; so is an option flag the action
cannot take, since only
.Ic fixup
and
.Ic merge
accept
.Fl C
or
.Fl c .
.Pp
.Ic C-c C-p ,
.Ic C-c C-r ,
.Ic C-c C-e ,
.Ic C-c C-s ,
.Ic C-c C-f
and
.Ic C-c C-d
rewrite the action of the current line to
.Ic pick , reword , edit , squash , fixup
or
.Ic drop
respectively; they refuse to touch comment,
.Ic exec
and other non-commit lines.
Rewriting a
.Ic fixup
line that carries a
.Fl C
or
.Fl c
flag to any other action also deletes the flag, which only
.Ic fixup
accepts.
.Ic M-p
and
.Ic M-n
move the current line up and down, for reordering commits.
The same commands are available as
.Ic M-x git-rebase-pick , git-rebase-reword , git-rebase-edit ,
.Ic git-rebase-squash , git-rebase-fixup , git-rebase-drop ,
.Ic git-rebase-move-line-up
and
.Ic git-rebase-move-line-down .
.Pp
As in commit buffers,
.Ic C-c C-c
saves the todo list and exits with status 0 so the rebase proceeds, and
.Ic C-c C-k
prompts for confirmation, then exits without saving and with a non-zero
status, making git abort the rebase.
All these bindings are built in, only active in rebase buffers, and
take priority over user Lisp bindings for the same keys.
.Ss Dired Mode
.Ic M-x dired
and
.Ic C-x d
prompt for a directory and list it in a read-only buffer, shown as
.Dq Dired
in the mode line.
The prompt is the ordinary path picker, where
.Ic Enter
on a directory descends into it rather than submitting it.
There are three ways to name a directory rather than descend into it:
.Ic M-Enter
submits the typed text literally (a deliberate deviation from Emacs),
appending
.Sq /\&.
and pressing
.Ic Enter
does the same and is what Emacs itself does, and answering with
something inside the directory works too, since a non-directory answer
means the directory containing it.
An empty answer means the directory of the current buffer.
.Ic C-x C-f
on a directory lists it as well, reusing the listing buffer for that
directory if one is already open, and so does naming a directory on the
command line
.Pq Ic kg src .
.Pp
The listing buffer is named
.Pa *Dired: /abs/path* ,
and that name is the mode's only state: two directories listed at once
are simply two buffers.
Row 0 is a header naming the directory; every row after it is one entry
behind a two-column mark gutter, with directories suffixed
.Sq / .
Entries are sorted with
.Xr strcoll 3 ,
hidden files are listed, and
.Sq \&.
and
.Sq \&..
are omitted, the header line and
.Ic ^
covering them.
The header is dimmed, directory names are colored as keywords, and a
mark or flag in the gutter is colored where it stands.
.Pp
.Bl -column "C-Home / C-End" "XXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXX" -compact
.It Sy Key Ta Sy Action
.It Enter Ta Visit the entry at point (dired-find-file)
.It ^ Ta List the parent directory (dired-up-directory)
.It g Ta Re-read the directory (dired-revert)
.It n / p Ta Move to the next / previous line
.It m Ta Mark the entry with * (dired-mark)
.It d Ta Flag the entry with D (dired-flag-file-deletion)
.It u Ta Clear the mark or flag (dired-unmark)
.It x Ta Delete the flagged entries (dired-do-flagged-delete)
.It q Ta Close the listing
.El
.Pp
.Ic Enter
on a directory lists it in place of the current listing; on anything
else it opens the entry exactly as
.Ic C-x C-f
would, so an entry removed behind
.Nm Ns 's
back reports the usual open error and
.Ic g
recovers.
A symbolic link to a directory is descended into; a broken one lists,
and opens, as a plain file.
.Ic m ,
.Ic d
and
.Ic u
write their marker into column 0 and step down one line, so a run of
entries takes one keystroke each, and
.Ic u
clears either marker.
The mark and the flag are distinct, as in Emacs:
.Ic x
acts on the
.Sq D
flags only, after a single
.Dq Delete N flagged entries? (y/n)
confirmation.
Each flagged entry's identity is recorded before that question is asked
and checked again at the deletion, against a descriptor held open on the
listed directory for the whole operation.
An entry that has since been removed, replaced by a different object, or
changed kind is skipped and counted, never deleted in the confirmed
entry's place.
Deletion is never recursive and never follows a symbolic link: a
directory is removed as a directory, so a non-empty one fails and says
so, a symbolic link is unlinked as the link it is even where the listing
marks it with a
.Sq /
because it points at a directory, and everything else is unlinked.
A failure does not stop the rest; the counts that failed and that were
skipped for having changed are reported, with the first error seen, and
the listing is re-read afterwards.
.Pp
Marks are the buffer's own text rather than editor state, so they take
no undo record, never make the buffer modified, and are dropped by
.Ic g
along with the listing that carried them.
In this version the
.Sq *
mark is inert: the commands that would consume it (copy, rename,
deleting the marked entries) are not implemented yet, and only the
.Sq D
flag has an action.
Copy, rename, mkdir and unmark-all are missing for the same reason,
along with
.Xr ls 1 Ns -style
size and permission columns.
.Pp
Every listing key is an
.Ic M-x
command as well:
.Ic M-x dired , dired-find-file , dired-up-directory , dired-revert ,
.Ic dired-mark , dired-flag-file-deletion , dired-unmark
and
.Ic dired-do-flagged-delete ;
all but the first refuse politely outside a listing.
The listing keys are built in rather than Lisp bindings, since user
bindings are
.Ic C-c
sequences by design, so nothing can shadow them and the mode is
identical in a
.Li WITH_LISP=0
build.
.Ss YAML Mode
Files ending in
.Pa .yaml
or
.Pa .yml
use YAML syntax highlighting automatically;
.Ic M-x yaml-mode
enables it manually for the current buffer, e.g. for an extensionless
file.
Highlighting is lexical and does not validate YAML: it colors mapping
keys, comments, quoted scalars, block scalars
.Pq Sq \&|
and
.Pq Sq >
(including across blank lines, tracking the block's indentation),
booleans/null, numbers, and structural markers such as
.Sq --- ,
.Sq ... ,
and
.Sq - ,
plus anchors, aliases and tags.
.Sh AUTOCOMPLETE
When electric pairing is enabled,
.Nm
automatically inserts matching closing brackets when an opening bracket is
typed, provided the cursor is at the end of a line.
Recognised pairs are
.Sq () ,
.Sq [] ,
and
.Sq {} .
Autocomplete is suppressed when text arrives quickly, such as during a paste
operation.
.Pp
Electric pairing is off by default.
Toggle it with
.Ic M-x electric-pair-mode ,
or enable it at startup by adding
.Ql (command-execute \(aqelectric-pair-mode)
to the Lisp init file (see
.Sx FILES ) .
.Sh GOTO LINE
.Ic M-g g
(or
.Ic M-g M-g )
prompts for a line number, optionally followed by a colon and a column
number
.Pq e.g. Ar 42:10 .
The cursor is moved to that position and the view is centred vertically.
.Pp
.Ic M-g
is a prefix map, in the Emacs style: further
.Ic M-g Ar key
bindings (e.g.
.Ic M-g n Ns / Ns Ic M-g p
and their Meta spellings
.Ic M-g M-n Ns / Ns Ic M-g M-p
for
.Ic next-error Ns / Ns Ic previous-error )
live under it rather than taking a key of their own.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Nm
uses the controlling terminal
.Pa /dev/tty
for raw-mode input and output.
Terminal size changes
.Pq SIGWINCH
are handled gracefully.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ev TERM
Names the terminal.
Read once, at startup, to decide whether
.Nm
asks for mouse reports at all; see
.Sx MOUSE .
Nothing else in
.Nm
consults it.
.It Ev KG_LISP_ARENA_BYTES
How many bytes of arena a
.Sq +lisp
build gives the interpreter, replacing the 10 MiB it is built with.
Plain bytes, or a
.Sq K
or
.Sq M
suffix for KiB or MiB:
.Sq 2M ,
.Sq 2048K
and
.Sq 2097152
are the same arena.
Read once, at startup.
Unset, empty, or anything that is not one of those spellings leaves the
compiled default alone.
A value that IS one of them but is below 655360 bytes is refused, naming
itself and that floor, and
.Nm
comes up with Lisp switched off rather than on a smaller arena than was
asked for; the floor is three times what the built-in Lisp prelude needs
to stay resident.
.Ic M-x lisp-arena-stats
reports the size in force.
Ignored by a
.Sq -lisp
build.
.It Ev KG_LSP_SERVER_C , Ev KG_LSP_SERVER_PYTHON , Ev KG_LSP_SERVER_GO , Ev KG_LSP_SERVER_RUST , Ev KG_LSP_SERVER_JAVA
The command line a
.Sq +lsp
build runs for that mode's language server, replacing the built-in
.Ic clangd ,
.Ic ty server ,
.Ic gopls ,
.Ic rust-analyzer
or
.Ic jdtls .
Run through
.Ic /bin/sh -c ,
so quoting and arguments work; unset or empty leaves the built-in.
A value beginning with
.Sq listen-hash:
and whitespace runs the rest as the command and talks to it over the TCP
socket that command announces, which is Oracle's nbcode; see
.Sx LSP .
Ignored by a
.Sq -lsp
build.
See
.Sx LSP .
.It Ev KG_LSP_TIMEOUT_MS
How long a
.Sq +lsp
build waits for one language-server request, in milliseconds, before
abandoning it and reporting so.
The default is 30000;
.Sq 0
waits forever, and anything that is not a number is ignored.
Read once per server, when it is started.
Ignored by a
.Sq -lsp
build.
See
.Sx LSP .
.It Ev KG_TS_GRAMMAR_PATH
Colon-separated list of directories a
.Sq +tree-sitter
build searches for grammar shared objects, before the path compiled into
the binary.
Each entry is a directory holding
.Pa libtree-sitter- Ns Ar name Ns Pa .so ;
an entry containing
.Sq %s
has the grammar name substituted into it first, so one entry can cover a
tree with one directory per grammar.
An empty or unset value means the compiled-in default.
Ignored by a
.Sq -tree-sitter
build.
.It Ev XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Base directory for the Lisp init file and package directory under
.Pa FILES
below;
.Pa ~/.config
when unset.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa /dev/tty
Terminal device used for raw-mode I/O.
.It Pa $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/kg/init.el
Lisp init file, loaded at startup unless
.Fl Q
is given.
Falls back to
.Pa ~/.config/kg/init.el .
.It Pa $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/kg/lisp/
Package directory searched by
.Li (load "name") .
.It Pa .kg-dap.json
Debug launch configurations, read from the nearest ancestor directory of
the file being debugged.
Trusted code; see
.Sx DEBUGGER .
.El
